WEBVTT

1
00:00:31,164 --> 00:00:37,037
[♪♪♪]

2
00:01:02,195 --> 00:01:08,068
[♪♪♪]

3
00:01:30,725 --> 00:01:36,598
[♪♪♪]

4
00:01:54,882 --> 00:01:56,784
[TIRES SCREECH]

5
00:02:02,824 --> 00:02:06,961
[CAR DOOR OPENS, CLOSES]

6
00:02:07,095 --> 00:02:12,600
[♪♪♪]

7
00:02:36,024 --> 00:02:38,059
[SPLASH]

8
00:02:42,830 --> 00:02:45,265
[TIRES SCREECH]

9
00:02:45,399 --> 00:02:48,201
[CAR REVS OFF]

10
00:02:53,708 --> 00:02:59,647
[♪♪♪]

11
00:03:24,806 --> 00:03:30,545
[♪♪♪]

12
00:03:33,246 --> 00:03:37,752
[BIRDS CHIRPING]

13
00:03:37,885 --> 00:03:43,758
[♪♪♪]

14
00:04:08,916 --> 00:04:14,789
[♪♪♪]

15
00:04:39,947 --> 00:04:45,853
[♪♪♪]

16
00:05:11,311 --> 00:05:16,851
[♪♪♪]

17
00:05:33,968 --> 00:05:36,804
[WHIRS]

18
00:05:38,438 --> 00:05:39,907
[BUZZING]

19
00:05:40,041 --> 00:05:43,711
[GUNSHOTS]

20
00:05:48,683 --> 00:05:50,218
GRAYSMITH:
Your hands are shaking.

21
00:05:50,350 --> 00:05:51,886
[SIGHS]

22
00:05:54,789 --> 00:05:55,923
Go ahead.

23
00:05:56,057 --> 00:05:57,158
Try again.

24
00:05:59,126 --> 00:06:01,394
Enough humiliation
for one day.

25
00:06:01,529 --> 00:06:03,363
I'm not cut out for this.

26
00:06:03,496 --> 00:06:04,966
Why are you lying to me?

27
00:06:05,099 --> 00:06:10,705
The grades haven't come out yet,
but I saw yours already.

28
00:06:10,838 --> 00:06:15,209
You scored higher than most
in every discipline,

29
00:06:15,343 --> 00:06:19,180
even higher than your superiors
when they were cadets.

30
00:06:19,313 --> 00:06:20,514
My teachers?

31
00:06:20,648 --> 00:06:21,949
Myself included.

32
00:06:23,718 --> 00:06:26,921
We've never had someone like you
at this academy.

33
00:06:27,054 --> 00:06:28,890
Why do I sense
this isn't a compliment?

34
00:06:31,826 --> 00:06:32,827
[BEEPS]

35
00:06:32,960 --> 00:06:34,362
[WHIRS]

36
00:06:34,494 --> 00:06:37,031
I can tell a bad shot
from a deliberate miss-aim.

37
00:06:38,733 --> 00:06:41,235
This shooting exam
is the last thing standing

38
00:06:41,369 --> 00:06:42,737
between you and the field,

39
00:06:42,870 --> 00:06:44,672
and I believe I know
what you're doing.

40
00:06:44,805 --> 00:06:47,275
You're self-sabotaging
your own score.

41
00:06:47,407 --> 00:06:49,243
Why would I do that?
Because you're scared

42
00:06:49,377 --> 00:06:51,612
about what's waiting for you
on the other side.

43
00:06:53,281 --> 00:06:55,716
You've been studying evil
for years,

44
00:06:55,850 --> 00:06:58,619
but you had theory
to protect you.

45
00:06:58,753 --> 00:07:00,922
Once you pass this exam,

46
00:07:01,055 --> 00:07:07,929
you're out there on the field
on your own, and you're scared.

47
00:07:08,062 --> 00:07:10,631
You're just beginning
to realize that you're not ready

48
00:07:10,765 --> 00:07:13,834
to confront what real evil
looks like.

49
00:07:13,968 --> 00:07:15,403
Forensics needs me here.

50
00:07:15,536 --> 00:07:17,772
Murder victims
need you, too.

51
00:07:17,905 --> 00:07:20,440
And how are you gonna help them
from behind a desk?

52
00:07:20,574 --> 00:07:22,109
My place is at the academy.

53
00:07:22,243 --> 00:07:24,477
And train others
for the rest of your life?

54
00:07:24,612 --> 00:07:26,213
[SCOFFS]
No.

55
00:07:26,347 --> 00:07:28,683
I refuse to let talent
like yours go to waste.

56
00:07:29,917 --> 00:07:32,853
All due respect, I gotta go.

57
00:07:34,221 --> 00:07:35,488
You better start
taking a real chance

58
00:07:35,623 --> 00:07:38,759
on yourself
before someone else does.

59
00:07:39,961 --> 00:07:41,429
I'll give you one shot.

60
00:07:43,130 --> 00:07:46,901
This next bullet either lands
you your first case

61
00:07:47,034 --> 00:07:50,271
or discharges you before your
field career has even begun.

62
00:07:51,605 --> 00:07:52,807
Fire accordingly.

63
00:07:57,411 --> 00:07:58,478
[CLICKS GUN]

64
00:08:06,654 --> 00:08:07,621
[GUNSHOT]

65
00:08:10,490 --> 00:08:13,961
[DOORS OPEN]

66
00:08:20,167 --> 00:08:21,769
[ELEVATOR BELL DINGS]

67
00:08:21,902 --> 00:08:25,206
[ELEVATOR DOORS SLIDE OPEN]

68
00:08:30,678 --> 00:08:31,812
Oh, oh, wait.

69
00:08:33,981 --> 00:08:35,049
Uh, 12.

70
00:08:44,125 --> 00:08:45,326
Might wanna try vodka.

71
00:08:47,528 --> 00:08:48,562
It's harder to smell.

72
00:08:49,764 --> 00:08:51,866
[PHONE RINGING IN BACKGROUND]

73
00:08:51,999 --> 00:08:55,636
[♪♪♪]

74
00:08:58,706 --> 00:09:00,441
GRAYSMITH:
See that man right there?

75
00:09:03,544 --> 00:09:06,047
Some would say his political
authority is absolute.

76
00:09:07,581 --> 00:09:09,083
Why am I here?

77
00:09:09,216 --> 00:09:12,586
Yesterday, his two
twin daughters disappeared.

78
00:09:12,720 --> 00:09:14,755
They found a body
a few hours ago

79
00:09:14,889 --> 00:09:17,324
face down in a river,
murdered.

80
00:09:17,458 --> 00:09:20,661
She was 14.

81
00:09:20,795 --> 00:09:23,064
ALAN:
But the other sister
is still missing.

82
00:09:23,197 --> 00:09:25,266
He talked
to the local police,

83
00:09:25,399 --> 00:09:28,269
got it to be
a co-investigation, private.

84
00:09:28,402 --> 00:09:29,870
No outside interference.

85
00:09:30,004 --> 00:09:31,105
When would this start?

86
00:09:31,238 --> 00:09:32,406
GRAYSMITH:
It already has.

87
00:09:34,041 --> 00:09:35,042
But?

88
00:09:35,176 --> 00:09:36,744
But he believes this case

89
00:09:36,877 --> 00:09:38,979
deserves more
of a personal touch.

90
00:09:39,113 --> 00:09:40,147
Is that why he's here?

91
00:09:41,550 --> 00:09:42,450
GRAYSMITH:
Yes.

92
00:09:43,918 --> 00:09:45,252
I know what you're gonna say.

93
00:09:45,386 --> 00:09:46,654
[DROPS FILE]

94
00:09:48,557 --> 00:09:52,259
I read your petition,
and if these really are

95
00:09:52,393 --> 00:09:55,796
your last few months,
who wouldn't wanna spend it

96
00:09:55,930 --> 00:09:57,532
safely behind a desk?

97
00:09:57,665 --> 00:09:58,933
Glad you understand.

98
00:09:59,066 --> 00:10:01,268
I need you to lead this case.

99
00:10:01,402 --> 00:10:04,472
Study the crime scene,
analyze all the clues,

100
00:10:04,605 --> 00:10:07,141
find the kidnapper,
bring back the missing child

101
00:10:07,274 --> 00:10:08,442
before it's too late.

102
00:10:08,577 --> 00:10:09,578
I'm sorry.

103
00:10:10,978 --> 00:10:13,247
I misunderstood.

104
00:10:13,380 --> 00:10:15,416
I thought I was here
for a different offer.

105
00:10:19,854 --> 00:10:21,956
I hope you find the right person
for the job,

106
00:10:23,390 --> 00:10:25,693
somebody with a little less
baggage.

107
00:10:25,826 --> 00:10:27,661
I'll sign
your retirement papers.

108
00:10:29,797 --> 00:10:31,332
You need my signature.

109
00:10:31,465 --> 00:10:32,800
This case is your ticket out.

110
00:10:32,933 --> 00:10:35,803
You take it, and I'll make sure
it's your last.

111
00:10:41,842 --> 00:10:42,943
Erica--
GRAYSMITH: Look, I know

112
00:10:43,077 --> 00:10:44,145
what you're going through.

113
00:10:44,278 --> 00:10:46,413
And trust me, we miss him too,

114
00:10:46,548 --> 00:10:49,049
but a single case
can't be enough

115
00:10:49,183 --> 00:10:51,620
to dismantle an entire career.

116
00:10:51,785 --> 00:10:53,921
He'll be in here any moment,

117
00:10:54,054 --> 00:10:55,689
and what do you want me
to tell him?

118
00:10:55,823 --> 00:10:57,458
They're letting you
send in one man?

119
00:10:57,592 --> 00:10:58,959
GRAYSMITH:
You won't be alone.

120
00:10:59,093 --> 00:11:01,428
There's a woman in the agency.

121
00:11:01,563 --> 00:11:03,130
They're enforcing assistance?

122
00:11:03,264 --> 00:11:04,732
I'm offering assistance.

123
00:11:04,865 --> 00:11:06,367
Whoever she is,
I don't need her.

124
00:11:06,500 --> 00:11:09,770
Her two degrees in forensic
psychology beg to differ.

125
00:11:09,904 --> 00:11:10,971
ALAN:
Erica--

126
00:11:11,105 --> 00:11:12,740
I'll join you there
as soon as I can,

127
00:11:12,873 --> 00:11:14,875
but for now,
there's a train that leaves

128
00:11:15,009 --> 00:11:19,079
in 60 minutes, and I'm intending
for you both to be on it.

129
00:11:19,213 --> 00:11:21,882
So are you in or out?

130
00:11:22,016 --> 00:11:23,284
[DOOR OPENS]

131
00:11:28,455 --> 00:11:29,890
[SIGHS]

132
00:11:30,024 --> 00:11:34,663
Detective, I don't want
my daughter's last memory to be

133
00:11:34,795 --> 00:11:37,064
of a father
who couldn't save her.

134
00:11:39,033 --> 00:11:41,035
I need to know
that I tried everything.

135
00:11:42,970 --> 00:11:44,071
Please.

136
00:11:45,507 --> 00:11:46,675
What would you do?

137
00:12:04,491 --> 00:12:06,026
[TWISTS CAP]

138
00:12:10,130 --> 00:12:13,934
[INDISTINCT CHATTER
IN BACKGROUND]

139
00:12:27,682 --> 00:12:29,250
AMELIA:
You look so different
in pictures.

140
00:12:31,085 --> 00:12:33,287
Says here you've been at it
for 20 years,

141
00:12:33,420 --> 00:12:34,755
10 of them working alone.

142
00:12:36,423 --> 00:12:38,727
Now you've got yourself
a brand-new partner.

143
00:12:38,859 --> 00:12:39,960
We're not partners.

144
00:12:40,094 --> 00:12:41,596
AMELIA:
Yet you haven't spoken

145
00:12:41,730 --> 00:12:43,497
to me since we got here,
no word of warning or advice,

146
00:12:43,632 --> 00:12:47,301
so I can't tell if you're either
too relaxed to have me here

147
00:12:47,434 --> 00:12:50,237
or too worried.

148
00:12:50,371 --> 00:12:52,439
I'm a criminologist,
not a babysitter.

149
00:12:53,907 --> 00:12:55,476
Did I do something to offend?

150
00:12:57,344 --> 00:13:00,447
Graysmith thinks having you here
will solve the case quicker,

151
00:13:00,582 --> 00:13:02,449
maybe prevent me
from making a mess.

152
00:13:02,584 --> 00:13:04,952
Now, I agree
with the second part,

153
00:13:05,085 --> 00:13:07,321
but what she thinks
we have in common,

154
00:13:07,454 --> 00:13:09,123
I don't know that yet.

155
00:13:09,256 --> 00:13:10,491
We've both chosen
to take murder

156
00:13:10,625 --> 00:13:12,226
and make it our business.

157
00:13:12,359 --> 00:13:13,561
You track killers;
I diagnose them.

158
00:13:13,695 --> 00:13:14,962
Same sickness, different angle.

159
00:13:15,095 --> 00:13:17,364
You know nothing about
diagnosing people.

160
00:13:22,236 --> 00:13:24,438
Right heel cap
on your shoe is torn up,

161
00:13:24,572 --> 00:13:26,508
shallow bite marks,
wide spacing.

162
00:13:26,641 --> 00:13:29,443
Large breed of dog, I'd say,
based on the bite.

163
00:13:29,577 --> 00:13:32,980
Maybe you adopted one recently
for companionship

164
00:13:33,113 --> 00:13:36,417
after losing
a friend or a family member.

165
00:13:39,621 --> 00:13:42,122
Do you always carry the photo
or is it a--

166
00:13:42,256 --> 00:13:44,626
a ritual of sorts
before you begin a case?

167
00:13:46,193 --> 00:13:49,229
Happens all the time
after breakups.

168
00:13:50,831 --> 00:13:52,534
People don't get rid
of an old photo,

169
00:13:52,667 --> 00:13:56,337
but they'll fold over the parts
that hurt the most.

170
00:13:59,239 --> 00:14:00,841
They wanna remember the rest.

171
00:14:03,844 --> 00:14:04,912
What was he like?

172
00:14:09,116 --> 00:14:10,150
We haven't even
left the station,

173
00:14:10,284 --> 00:14:12,620
but I can tell already.

174
00:14:12,754 --> 00:14:14,221
Business and feelings,
they don't go well in this

175
00:14:14,355 --> 00:14:15,523
line of work.

176
00:14:15,657 --> 00:14:17,257
Okay?

177
00:14:17,391 --> 00:14:19,527
Okay.
ALAN: Yeah?

178
00:14:19,661 --> 00:14:21,161
You don't have
to worry about me.

179
00:14:21,295 --> 00:14:22,463
I'm here for research,
pure and simple.

180
00:14:22,597 --> 00:14:24,331
ALAN:
Good.

181
00:14:24,465 --> 00:14:28,536
I think the question is,
why would Graysmith give you

182
00:14:28,670 --> 00:14:30,003
<i>this</i> case?

183
00:14:32,306 --> 00:14:35,876
[TRAIN PASSING BY]

184
00:14:42,650 --> 00:14:46,186
[TRAIN HORN BLARES]

185
00:14:46,320 --> 00:14:47,722
AMELIA:
Where do we find him?

186
00:14:47,856 --> 00:14:49,624
ALAN:
I'm hoping he finds us.

187
00:14:49,758 --> 00:14:52,527
AMELIA:
Your suede coat
was a great choice.

188
00:14:52,660 --> 00:14:55,062
You really don't smile,
do you?

189
00:14:55,195 --> 00:14:59,466
Alan Kramer?
[CAMERA SHUTTER CLICKING]

190
00:14:59,601 --> 00:15:00,769
Can I help you?

191
00:15:00,901 --> 00:15:02,504
Hey, hey.

192
00:15:02,637 --> 00:15:05,139
Hold onto the negatives.

193
00:15:05,272 --> 00:15:06,541
ALAN:
You greet all
new arrivals this way?

194
00:15:06,674 --> 00:15:08,409
Only the ones worth
talking about.
Huh.

195
00:15:08,543 --> 00:15:09,611
Alan Kramer?
Yes, sir.

196
00:15:09,744 --> 00:15:11,613
Hank Brewster.
Lieutenant.

197
00:15:11,746 --> 00:15:13,715
It's hard not to recognize
a face like yours, though.

198
00:15:13,848 --> 00:15:14,783
I can hardly believe
you're here.

199
00:15:14,915 --> 00:15:16,083
Right.

200
00:15:16,216 --> 00:15:17,451
This your first time
in Canton, ma'am?

201
00:15:17,585 --> 00:15:19,052
I don't travel much.

202
00:15:19,186 --> 00:15:21,488
Well, I fear you'll be surprised
in all the worst ways.

203
00:15:21,623 --> 00:15:23,257
I don't know how much they told
you before you left, but--

204
00:15:23,390 --> 00:15:24,458
Not much.
We left in a hurry.

205
00:15:24,592 --> 00:15:27,027
All right.
This is Arthur Otto.

206
00:15:27,161 --> 00:15:30,832
He's a senator, philanthropist,
family man.

207
00:15:30,964 --> 00:15:32,600
Most of the buildings
in this town were rescued

208
00:15:32,734 --> 00:15:34,067
through his charity.

209
00:15:34,201 --> 00:15:36,003
Politicians dream
of a reputation like that.

210
00:15:36,136 --> 00:15:37,971
This guy's actually earned it.

211
00:15:38,105 --> 00:15:40,207
AMELIA:
He's financing
an additional investigation?

212
00:15:40,340 --> 00:15:41,876
Yeah, no expense spared.

213
00:15:42,009 --> 00:15:43,745
His campaign's recruited
hundreds of volunteers,

214
00:15:43,878 --> 00:15:46,514
organized search parties,
but after this morning's news--

215
00:15:46,648 --> 00:15:48,282
It's no longer enough.

216
00:15:48,415 --> 00:15:50,117
By the way, I took the
trouble to get you guys set up.

217
00:15:50,250 --> 00:15:52,654
Your case files and bags
are in your rooms.

218
00:15:52,787 --> 00:15:54,556
How far is the police station
from the hotel?

219
00:15:54,689 --> 00:15:56,323
Any closer,
you'd be sleeping in my office.

220
00:15:58,058 --> 00:15:59,159
Now, I've asked them
to leave the body

221
00:15:59,293 --> 00:16:00,994
on site until your arrival.

222
00:16:01,128 --> 00:16:03,297
And as of now,
all of our resources are

223
00:16:03,430 --> 00:16:04,766
at your disposal.

224
00:16:19,581 --> 00:16:22,382
You know, we still teach your
Belardo case at the academy.

225
00:16:22,517 --> 00:16:24,485
Mm-hmm.

226
00:16:24,619 --> 00:16:26,888
And you are?

227
00:16:27,020 --> 00:16:29,624
Amelia Pembrock, I'm with
the Behavioral Science Unit.

228
00:16:29,757 --> 00:16:31,693
I mean, uh, like,
what do you do?

229
00:16:31,826 --> 00:16:35,930
Oh, I trace criminal profiles
based on forensic patterns.

230
00:16:36,063 --> 00:16:38,666
HANK: Yeah.
I am also a field agent.

231
00:16:38,800 --> 00:16:39,968
HANK:
Have you worked many cases?

232
00:16:40,100 --> 00:16:42,202
No. This is my first.

233
00:16:57,819 --> 00:17:02,524
[INDISTINCT RADIO CHATTER]

234
00:17:13,568 --> 00:17:17,371
The river usually washes down
dead cattle, tree branches,

235
00:17:17,505 --> 00:17:20,407
but this is not stuff
that we're used to seeing.

236
00:17:20,542 --> 00:17:21,910
Ma'am, I gotta warn you that

237
00:17:22,042 --> 00:17:23,745
what you're about
to see cannot be unseen.

238
00:17:23,878 --> 00:17:25,412
I'll manage.

239
00:17:25,547 --> 00:17:28,048
[BACKGROUND RADIO CHATTER]
[TRAIN HORN BLARES]

240
00:17:28,181 --> 00:17:29,951
ALAN: Any suspects?
HANK: Nobody yet.

241
00:17:30,083 --> 00:17:33,021
Then we found a witness
who saw a man dump a body.

242
00:17:33,186 --> 00:17:35,723
He didn't report it at first,
because he thought it was a dog.

243
00:17:35,857 --> 00:17:37,357
Dog?

244
00:17:37,491 --> 00:17:39,493
Hunters up in the delta,
you can only fire a rifle next

245
00:17:39,627 --> 00:17:41,729
to a bloodhound so many times
before it goes deaf.

246
00:17:41,863 --> 00:17:43,798
Then the deaf dog
is a worthless dog.

247
00:17:43,932 --> 00:17:45,934
Tell me about the vehicle.

248
00:17:46,066 --> 00:17:48,536
Abandoned up
on Highway 61, fake plates.

249
00:17:48,670 --> 00:17:49,871
Couldn't pull any prints.

250
00:17:51,039 --> 00:17:52,574
You can forget about CCTV.

251
00:17:52,707 --> 00:17:54,008
It's broken.

252
00:17:54,141 --> 00:17:55,442
I talked to maintenance,
and they've been waiting

253
00:17:55,577 --> 00:17:57,411
on new electrical since March.

254
00:17:57,545 --> 00:17:59,047
Perfect timing.
Or maybe he knew.

255
00:17:59,179 --> 00:18:01,816
Who reported the body?
Fishermen.

256
00:18:01,950 --> 00:18:04,084
They saw fish eating away
at something under the surface

257
00:18:04,217 --> 00:18:05,687
of the water and--
That's when you found it.

258
00:18:05,820 --> 00:18:07,387
That's when we found
the first missing twin.

259
00:18:13,126 --> 00:18:14,662
Alan Kramer,
this is Colin Ripley.

260
00:18:14,796 --> 00:18:16,564
He's our medical examiner.

261
00:18:16,698 --> 00:18:17,932
How do you do?
ALAN: Hi.

262
00:18:18,066 --> 00:18:19,567
HANK:
Usually first on the scene.

263
00:18:19,701 --> 00:18:22,135
[SIGHS]
All right. Shall we?

264
00:18:22,269 --> 00:18:23,370
Yes, sir.

265
00:18:26,040 --> 00:18:27,742
[BIRDS CHIRPING]

266
00:18:27,875 --> 00:18:30,277
COLIN [SIGHS]:
Gretta Otto.

267
00:18:30,410 --> 00:18:32,647
Since the twins were identical,

268
00:18:32,780 --> 00:18:34,816
blood sample was the quickest
way to an ID.

269
00:18:34,949 --> 00:18:36,618
Body was underwater all night.

270
00:18:36,751 --> 00:18:39,087
Probably the worst place
to dump her.

271
00:18:39,252 --> 00:18:42,356
Whoever did this wanted
the current to take her away.

272
00:18:42,489 --> 00:18:44,025
But it didn't.

273
00:18:44,157 --> 00:18:45,526
You think it might be
intentional?

274
00:18:45,660 --> 00:18:46,828
Intentional?

275
00:18:46,961 --> 00:18:48,529
Water that dirty could
be corrosive,

276
00:18:48,663 --> 00:18:50,163
wash away evidence.

277
00:18:50,297 --> 00:18:51,666
You said you checked the river
for the other body?

278
00:18:51,799 --> 00:18:53,001
HANK: We did, every inch of it.
Uh-huh.

279
00:18:53,133 --> 00:18:54,669
Could be ransom.

280
00:18:54,802 --> 00:18:57,437
Ransom criminals do target
the wealthy. But--

281
00:18:57,572 --> 00:18:59,807
If there's wealth in this town,
it's definitely the Ottos.

282
00:18:59,941 --> 00:19:01,643
But then why not contact
the family?

283
00:19:01,776 --> 00:19:03,176
If he wanted us
to find the body,

284
00:19:03,310 --> 00:19:05,412
he would've dumped it
on the family porch.

285
00:19:05,546 --> 00:19:06,748
So not a ransom.

286
00:19:06,881 --> 00:19:09,083
Why? Did you see a note?

287
00:19:09,216 --> 00:19:10,985
Did the witness provide
a description?

288
00:19:11,119 --> 00:19:12,352
We tried to do a composite,

289
00:19:12,486 --> 00:19:14,187
but it was too far away,
it was too dark.

290
00:19:14,321 --> 00:19:15,489
AMELIA:
Sure.

291
00:19:15,623 --> 00:19:17,391
If the media asks,
leave that part out.

292
00:19:17,525 --> 00:19:19,694
Tell 'em our witness
got a clear look at our guy.

293
00:19:19,827 --> 00:19:21,428
A clear look?
ALAN: Clear look.

294
00:19:21,562 --> 00:19:22,964
AMELIA:
That's right.
It could draw out the killer

295
00:19:23,097 --> 00:19:24,197
if we suggest he was spotted
in the area.

296
00:19:24,331 --> 00:19:25,533
MAN 1:
Coming back empty.

297
00:19:25,667 --> 00:19:27,035
Preliminary clues.
What about 'em?

298
00:19:27,167 --> 00:19:28,703
What makes you think
it's a homicide?

299
00:19:28,836 --> 00:19:30,038
Rope marks.

300
00:19:30,170 --> 00:19:31,471
Wrists, neck.

301
00:19:31,606 --> 00:19:33,173
They were either taken off after

302
00:19:33,306 --> 00:19:35,576
or washed away by the water,
but they were there.

303
00:19:35,710 --> 00:19:37,945
We also found tissue
under the fingernails.

304
00:19:38,079 --> 00:19:39,047
ALAN:
Flesh?

305
00:19:39,179 --> 00:19:40,280
It's too early to say.

306
00:19:40,414 --> 00:19:42,449
There's, uh, one other thing.

307
00:19:42,583 --> 00:19:44,217
It's pretty strange to see.

308
00:19:44,351 --> 00:19:45,887
[UNZIPS BAG]

309
00:19:46,020 --> 00:19:47,421
[BUZZING]

310
00:19:47,555 --> 00:19:49,023
COLIN:
Her teeth have been smashed.

311
00:19:49,157 --> 00:19:50,091
Likely the fall.

312
00:19:50,223 --> 00:19:51,659
Wasn't the fall.

313
00:19:51,793 --> 00:19:53,226
Look at her nose and her chin.

314
00:19:54,361 --> 00:19:56,196
No fractures or visible cuts.

315
00:19:58,633 --> 00:20:01,334
Rough landing would've bruised
more than just her mouth.

316
00:20:03,236 --> 00:20:04,872
Bridge was just a drop-off spot.

317
00:20:06,674 --> 00:20:08,241
There was blood on the rail.

318
00:20:08,375 --> 00:20:09,711
ALAN:
Blunt force trauma.

319
00:20:10,878 --> 00:20:12,345
Yeah, I saw that.

320
00:20:12,479 --> 00:20:14,414
COLIN:
All right then,
mugging gone wrong.

321
00:20:14,549 --> 00:20:16,416
Backpack was empty,
all contents taken.

322
00:20:16,551 --> 00:20:17,719
Takes her books
and her lunchbox,

323
00:20:17,852 --> 00:20:19,319
but leaves that gold bracelet?

324
00:20:19,453 --> 00:20:21,055
I-I don't think we can
consider robbery a motive.

325
00:20:21,189 --> 00:20:22,255
Yeah,
so why stage it as one?

326
00:20:22,389 --> 00:20:23,658
MAN 2:
We found something!

327
00:20:24,892 --> 00:20:25,827
What is it?

328
00:20:26,861 --> 00:20:27,729
Looks like a gun.

329
00:20:27,862 --> 00:20:28,763
A gun?

330
00:20:30,064 --> 00:20:31,966
I don't understand.

331
00:20:32,100 --> 00:20:34,401
He takes the risk
to drop her here,

332
00:20:34,535 --> 00:20:37,071
off the busiest bridge,
right under everyone's nose.

333
00:20:39,339 --> 00:20:40,340
Yeah.

334
00:20:42,375 --> 00:20:43,945
You can go ahead
and bring her up.

335
00:20:46,180 --> 00:20:47,782
All right,
let's bring her up, slowly,

336
00:20:47,915 --> 00:20:49,584
respectfully.

337
00:20:49,717 --> 00:20:51,052
There's a missing girl
out there.

338
00:20:51,185 --> 00:20:53,988
If she's alive, she's counting
on us to find her.

339
00:20:54,122 --> 00:20:57,792
[BIRDS CHIRPING]

340
00:20:57,925 --> 00:21:03,631
[♪♪♪]

341
00:21:07,400 --> 00:21:09,237
LAB ASSISTANT:
Rope marks confirm it.

342
00:21:09,369 --> 00:21:10,938
She died of asphyxiation.

343
00:21:11,072 --> 00:21:13,541
[CLICKS TONGUE]
It was intimate, personal.

344
00:21:13,674 --> 00:21:15,042
Tell 'em about the teeth.

345
00:21:15,176 --> 00:21:16,409
You were right.

346
00:21:16,544 --> 00:21:19,647
Trauma to the teeth
wasn't from the fall.

347
00:21:19,781 --> 00:21:22,349
Regular patterns, shaped,
purposeful.

348
00:21:22,482 --> 00:21:23,985
Meaning what?

349
00:21:24,118 --> 00:21:25,452
Inflicted separately?

350
00:21:25,586 --> 00:21:29,157
After the murder,
but before dumping the body.

351
00:21:29,289 --> 00:21:31,726
What do you know
about forensic odontology?

352
00:21:31,859 --> 00:21:33,861
[SCRIBBLING]

353
00:21:33,995 --> 00:21:35,797
Call the town's dentist.

354
00:21:35,930 --> 00:21:37,765
He'll have some answers for you.

355
00:21:37,899 --> 00:21:39,133
The pieces of flesh?

356
00:21:39,267 --> 00:21:40,835
Under her fingernails?
Yeah.

357
00:21:40,968 --> 00:21:42,335
Not human.

358
00:21:42,469 --> 00:21:43,771
Chicken.
Chicken?

359
00:21:43,905 --> 00:21:45,006
Raw chicken.

360
00:21:45,139 --> 00:21:47,340
After hours underwater?

361
00:21:47,474 --> 00:21:49,544
Current will wash out
fibers that small.

362
00:21:49,677 --> 00:21:52,847
Apparently not fast enough.
LAB ASSISTANT: One last thing.

363
00:21:52,980 --> 00:21:55,348
There were traces of nitrate
on the victim's elbow.

364
00:21:55,482 --> 00:21:57,585
Your killer could have some
gunpowder residue.

365
00:21:57,718 --> 00:21:59,352
But no wounds consistent
with firearms.

366
00:21:59,486 --> 00:22:01,522
And no bullets
found on the scene.

367
00:22:01,656 --> 00:22:03,157
We don't know yet
how the gun they found fits

368
00:22:03,291 --> 00:22:04,158
in all this.

369
00:22:04,292 --> 00:22:06,060
ALAN:
Hm.

370
00:22:06,194 --> 00:22:08,129
AMELIA: Hm.
What are you thinking?

371
00:22:08,262 --> 00:22:10,531
Our man planned this carefully,

372
00:22:10,665 --> 00:22:12,934
studied the path
of the river,

373
00:22:13,067 --> 00:22:14,535
understands
the city's geography.

374
00:22:14,669 --> 00:22:16,469
A local.
Maybe.

375
00:22:16,604 --> 00:22:19,540
But then why throw a gun
at the crime scene?

376
00:22:21,474 --> 00:22:25,980
Lifetime of cases,
you scrape for one perfect piece

377
00:22:26,113 --> 00:22:29,382
of evidence, and now all
of a sudden we've got too much.

378
00:22:29,517 --> 00:22:32,286
This is the opposite
of a perfect crime.

379
00:22:32,419 --> 00:22:33,721
It's too easy.

380
00:22:33,855 --> 00:22:37,258
[THUNDER RUMBLING]

381
00:22:37,390 --> 00:22:38,425
[RAIN PATTERING]

382
00:22:38,559 --> 00:22:41,929
[THUNDER RUMBLING]

383
00:22:44,532 --> 00:22:47,568
ARTHUR:
You know what
frightens me the most?

384
00:22:47,702 --> 00:22:49,837
That I'll forget the sound
of their voices.

385
00:22:49,971 --> 00:22:52,039
Go home to your wife,
Arthur.

386
00:22:53,241 --> 00:22:54,742
When my daughters went missing,

387
00:22:56,077 --> 00:22:58,012
I went against
my lawyer's advice

388
00:22:59,347 --> 00:23:00,815
and I came straight to you.

389
00:23:02,449 --> 00:23:03,483
You wanna know why?

390
00:23:05,820 --> 00:23:06,988
'Cause you're the best.

391
00:23:08,823 --> 00:23:12,425
All those victims
you've rescued,

392
00:23:12,560 --> 00:23:16,931
all of their stories are
now baked into your legacy.

393
00:23:19,901 --> 00:23:26,540
Remember what you once told me,
"You are who you save."

394
00:23:26,674 --> 00:23:28,609
[CLICKS TONGUE]
I understand how difficult it is

395
00:23:28,743 --> 00:23:30,845
trusting others
with your daughters' life.

396
00:23:34,548 --> 00:23:38,286
But can a marriage survive
the death of a child,

397
00:23:39,954 --> 00:23:41,222
let alone two?

398
00:23:42,590 --> 00:23:43,724
She's alive.

399
00:23:44,792 --> 00:23:46,560
Try to have faith, Arthur.

400
00:23:48,930 --> 00:23:51,299
I just don't know how.

401
00:23:53,734 --> 00:23:58,406
Those people that I sent,
they're trained for this.

402
00:23:58,539 --> 00:24:01,542
They'll piece together
the clues, solve the case,

403
00:24:01,676 --> 00:24:02,743
and find her.

404
00:24:03,844 --> 00:24:06,047
I know they'll find her.

405
00:24:06,180 --> 00:24:08,215
I just don't know
if it'll be quick enough.

406
00:24:09,482 --> 00:24:11,319
Go to Canton.

407
00:24:11,451 --> 00:24:12,820
Your team needs you.

408
00:24:12,954 --> 00:24:16,791
[BIRDS CHIRPING]

409
00:24:32,873 --> 00:24:37,345
[CAR DOORS OPEN AND CLOSE]

410
00:24:37,477 --> 00:24:39,647
Once inside, I have
to show her some photos.

411
00:24:39,780 --> 00:24:41,782
Photos of what?
The girl's body?

412
00:24:41,916 --> 00:24:43,217
Some things only a mother
would know.

413
00:24:43,351 --> 00:24:45,319
Are we sure it's the best time?

414
00:24:45,453 --> 00:24:46,721
Why,
you know a better time?

415
00:24:49,423 --> 00:24:50,524
20 minutes max.

416
00:24:52,994 --> 00:24:54,929
ILSA:
My husband said
you'd be coming.

417
00:24:55,062 --> 00:24:56,697
What do you remember about
the hours leading up

418
00:24:56,831 --> 00:24:58,332
to the disappearance?

419
00:24:58,466 --> 00:25:00,167
ILSA:
It was a day like any other.

420
00:25:00,301 --> 00:25:02,470
They left school,
and they were supposed

421
00:25:02,603 --> 00:25:03,771
to come right home.

422
00:25:03,904 --> 00:25:05,539
ALAN: But they didn't.
Mm.

423
00:25:05,673 --> 00:25:08,809
Took me hours to realize
that something was wrong.

424
00:25:08,943 --> 00:25:10,678
Did they always walk back?

425
00:25:10,811 --> 00:25:12,013
ILSA:
Sometimes.

426
00:25:12,146 --> 00:25:13,347
It's really far.

427
00:25:13,481 --> 00:25:16,050
Yeah, but they did everything
together,

428
00:25:16,183 --> 00:25:18,252
one always keeping an eye out
for the other.

429
00:25:18,386 --> 00:25:20,588
AMELIA:
So you never felt
it was dangerous?

430
00:25:20,721 --> 00:25:24,458
No. Things like this might be
common in big cities, but here,

431
00:25:24,592 --> 00:25:26,961
crime is nonexistent.

432
00:25:27,094 --> 00:25:30,398
Was their last name
ever a problem?

433
00:25:30,531 --> 00:25:32,500
No. Why?

434
00:25:32,633 --> 00:25:34,435
Are you thinking this
is some kind of ransom?

435
00:25:34,568 --> 00:25:36,470
Mrs. Otto.
Ilsa.

436
00:25:36,604 --> 00:25:41,510
We think a case of this nature
is of personal matter.

437
00:25:41,642 --> 00:25:43,411
Do you know anybody that would
have a grudge against

438
00:25:43,544 --> 00:25:46,047
your husband or an interest
in your daughters?

439
00:25:46,180 --> 00:25:47,848
No.

440
00:25:47,982 --> 00:25:49,750
ALAN: No.
No.

441
00:25:49,884 --> 00:25:50,985
Everybody knew them.

442
00:25:52,553 --> 00:25:55,356
They're sweet girls,
and they're so polite,

443
00:25:56,690 --> 00:25:58,559
and they're very
hardworking, too.

444
00:25:58,692 --> 00:26:00,194
What type of work?

445
00:26:00,327 --> 00:26:03,764
Community jobs,
after school activities.

446
00:26:03,898 --> 00:26:08,169
They worked at the medical lab
and the butcher shop.

447
00:26:08,302 --> 00:26:10,505
For their age, that's a little--
It's-It's unusual.

448
00:26:10,638 --> 00:26:12,173
Yeah, I know.

449
00:26:12,306 --> 00:26:14,975
It's just Arthur
really encouraged the girls

450
00:26:15,109 --> 00:26:16,277
to get involved.

451
00:26:19,080 --> 00:26:22,850
I-It's not easy, you know,
for a child to grow up

452
00:26:22,983 --> 00:26:27,021
and feel normal when all they've
ever known is wealth.

453
00:26:29,356 --> 00:26:31,625
My daughter experienced
more pain

454
00:26:31,759 --> 00:26:35,463
in a single night than I have
in my entire life,

455
00:26:38,065 --> 00:26:41,902
and she was a--
a good girl, Detective.

456
00:26:43,270 --> 00:26:44,939
She was so good.

457
00:26:45,072 --> 00:26:47,408
What does a 14-year-old
have to do

458
00:26:47,542 --> 00:26:49,578
to deserve something like this?

459
00:26:49,710 --> 00:26:51,812
She died face down in a river.

460
00:26:53,548 --> 00:26:57,485
She was alone,
and she was just waiting

461
00:26:57,618 --> 00:26:59,653
on somebody from home
to come get her.

462
00:27:03,124 --> 00:27:06,026
[CRIES]

463
00:27:06,160 --> 00:27:07,094
Look,

464
00:27:12,233 --> 00:27:13,834
I had to sign
the death certificate

465
00:27:13,968 --> 00:27:15,302
on my own child today.

466
00:27:17,471 --> 00:27:19,541
My other daughter
is still out there.

467
00:27:19,673 --> 00:27:22,343
She's missing, and you're here.

468
00:27:23,677 --> 00:27:25,246
I know
what you're going through.

469
00:27:26,981 --> 00:27:27,948
Do you?

470
00:27:29,150 --> 00:27:30,451
Do you have children?

471
00:27:34,788 --> 00:27:35,689
[DOOR OPENS]

472
00:27:35,823 --> 00:27:40,327
[HEELS CLACKING]

473
00:27:40,461 --> 00:27:41,495
[DOOR CLOSES]

474
00:27:43,731 --> 00:27:45,199
Did you get everything
you need?

475
00:27:47,001 --> 00:27:49,069
Yeah, enough to get started.

476
00:27:49,203 --> 00:27:51,205
Captain Graysmith
has great admiration

477
00:27:51,338 --> 00:27:52,541
for your history,

478
00:27:52,673 --> 00:27:54,975
says you are
a fine investigator.

479
00:27:55,109 --> 00:27:57,044
And for that reason,
my wife and I,

480
00:27:57,178 --> 00:28:00,447
we will follow your decisions,
whatever it takes,

481
00:28:00,582 --> 00:28:02,551
just so long as you bring
our daughter back.

482
00:28:02,683 --> 00:28:03,652
Yes, sir.

483
00:28:03,784 --> 00:28:04,685
We'll do our best.

484
00:28:07,021 --> 00:28:09,857
[FOOTSTEPS RECEDING]

485
00:28:11,825 --> 00:28:14,128
AMELIA:
I know Canton somehow
doesn't have a digital archive,

486
00:28:14,261 --> 00:28:17,164
but are you sure the library
is the best place to start?

487
00:28:17,298 --> 00:28:18,533
[PEN CLICKING]

488
00:28:18,667 --> 00:28:20,067
It's quiet.

489
00:28:20,201 --> 00:28:22,537
I'm just surprised you
of all people don't want us out

490
00:28:22,671 --> 00:28:23,771
in the field.

491
00:28:23,904 --> 00:28:26,040
They're scouting
all the bridges,

492
00:28:26,173 --> 00:28:29,176
following the currents
from sunrise to sundown.

493
00:28:30,645 --> 00:28:32,313
They're not gonna miss
another body.

494
00:28:33,648 --> 00:28:34,915
What's this?

495
00:28:35,049 --> 00:28:37,418
It's old articles,
criminal history of the city.

496
00:28:38,852 --> 00:28:40,120
Barely has any.

497
00:28:40,254 --> 00:28:41,556
You looking for clues?

498
00:28:41,690 --> 00:28:43,357
Connections
to previous murders.

499
00:28:44,659 --> 00:28:46,227
What makes you think
there are any?

500
00:28:46,360 --> 00:28:48,028
AMELIA:
I don't,
but if we're looking

501
00:28:48,162 --> 00:28:50,565
for patterns,
this is where we'd find 'em.

502
00:28:50,699 --> 00:28:52,366
Ah.

503
00:28:52,499 --> 00:28:54,703
Okay, let's--
Let's say she's still alive.

504
00:28:56,571 --> 00:28:59,940
Why kidnap two girls
and only murder one?

505
00:29:00,074 --> 00:29:03,410
Brewster said
Gretta was the bigger twin,

506
00:29:03,545 --> 00:29:07,147
so maybe he got rid
of one sister to get

507
00:29:07,281 --> 00:29:08,717
to the other.

508
00:29:08,849 --> 00:29:10,484
Gotta stop saying he.

509
00:29:10,619 --> 00:29:14,121
We don't know
the suspect is male.

510
00:29:16,190 --> 00:29:21,328
Yeah, that's our killer,
a 95-pound female with a kink

511
00:29:21,462 --> 00:29:23,931
for little girls
who's fit enough

512
00:29:24,064 --> 00:29:27,167
to single-handedly lift a body
up and over a railing.

513
00:29:27,301 --> 00:29:28,769
All right,
you made your point.

514
00:29:31,839 --> 00:29:33,407
All right.

515
00:29:33,541 --> 00:29:35,309
I strangle you,
I tie you up,

516
00:29:35,442 --> 00:29:36,745
and I load you into
the back of a vehicle.

517
00:29:36,877 --> 00:29:38,812
Without leaving
any fingerprints.

518
00:29:38,946 --> 00:29:42,416
ALAN:
But why do I drop you off here
where anybody could see me?

519
00:29:42,550 --> 00:29:44,418
Because you're a moron.

520
00:29:44,552 --> 00:29:46,487
But I'm not a moron.

521
00:29:46,621 --> 00:29:48,289
I didn't leave a single
fingerprint.

522
00:29:50,057 --> 00:29:51,660
Okay.

523
00:29:51,792 --> 00:29:53,961
So you smash my teeth
at the last moment,

524
00:29:54,094 --> 00:29:55,829
dump my body.
ALAN: Mm.

525
00:29:55,963 --> 00:29:57,865
If it's not for torture--

526
00:29:57,998 --> 00:29:59,933
It's out of precaution.

527
00:30:00,067 --> 00:30:01,168
But why?

528
00:30:06,273 --> 00:30:08,976
You need to ask
a professional.

529
00:30:09,109 --> 00:30:12,346
ODONTOLOGIST [OVER PHONE]:
<i>Children's mouths</i>
<i>evolve quickly, drastically.</i>

530
00:30:12,479 --> 00:30:14,481
<i>I know this is crucial</i>
<i>to the investigation,</i>

531
00:30:14,616 --> 00:30:17,752
<i>but I fear reconstructing</i>
<i>a mold of her mouth</i>

532
00:30:17,885 --> 00:30:19,253
<i>will prove difficult.</i>

533
00:30:19,386 --> 00:30:20,588
How difficult?

534
00:30:20,722 --> 00:30:22,122
ODONTOLOGIST:
<i>Every set of teeth</i>

535
00:30:22,256 --> 00:30:23,457
<i>is one of a kind,</i>
<i>like a fingerprint.</i>

536
00:30:23,591 --> 00:30:26,026
Reconstruction can be done?

537
00:30:26,160 --> 00:30:28,128
FORENSIC ODONTOLOGIST:
<i>With patience, yes.</i>

538
00:30:28,262 --> 00:30:31,700
<i>Working off past records</i>
<i>could take days.</i>

539
00:30:31,832 --> 00:30:33,867
All right.
Call me when you have something.

540
00:30:38,640 --> 00:30:40,575
GRAYSMITH:
Wasn't easy
to secure this meeting.

541
00:30:40,709 --> 00:30:42,376
AMELIA [OVER PHONE]:
<i>Think we can trust him?</i>

542
00:30:42,510 --> 00:30:45,212
Guy's a fox.
Best reporter in town.

543
00:30:46,980 --> 00:30:48,817
GRAYSMITH [OVER PHONE]:
<i>Trade information carefully.</i>

544
00:30:48,949 --> 00:30:50,050
Ma'am?

545
00:30:50,184 --> 00:30:51,786
Yes?

546
00:30:51,919 --> 00:30:53,887
AMELIA [OVER PHONE]:
<i>Thank you for the opportunity.</i>

547
00:30:54,021 --> 00:30:55,690
Work as a team, Pembrock.

548
00:30:55,824 --> 00:30:58,092
Don't take shortcuts.

549
00:30:58,225 --> 00:31:00,762
Close this case,
no matter the cost.

550
00:31:02,329 --> 00:31:03,397
[PHONE BEEPS]

551
00:31:05,366 --> 00:31:08,235
[BIRDS CAWING]

552
00:31:08,369 --> 00:31:09,671
Since yesterday, police

553
00:31:09,804 --> 00:31:11,673
and press have received
hundreds of tips.

554
00:31:11,806 --> 00:31:13,040
ALAN:
Let me guess.

555
00:31:13,173 --> 00:31:14,375
All anonymous.

556
00:31:14,509 --> 00:31:15,777
Phones won't stop ringing.

557
00:31:15,909 --> 00:31:17,746
Everybody wants to report
on rivals, neighbors,

558
00:31:17,878 --> 00:31:19,012
even family members,

559
00:31:19,146 --> 00:31:21,516
anybody with strange
sexual habits.

560
00:31:21,649 --> 00:31:23,917
Some are calling claiming they
committed the murder themselves.

561
00:31:24,051 --> 00:31:24,918
You know they're not guilty.

562
00:31:25,052 --> 00:31:26,420
That's right.

563
00:31:26,554 --> 00:31:28,556
Nobody can provide us
with new information.

564
00:31:28,690 --> 00:31:30,257
Everything they say
is public already.

565
00:31:30,391 --> 00:31:32,761
You think the killer's
in it for the press?

566
00:31:32,893 --> 00:31:34,094
He could be tracking
the investigation

567
00:31:34,228 --> 00:31:35,764
from the inside, yeah.

568
00:31:35,896 --> 00:31:37,331
AMELIA:
Making sure
it doesn't get too close to him.

569
00:31:37,464 --> 00:31:39,534
Mm-hmm.
So all we need is a name.

570
00:31:40,802 --> 00:31:41,969
I don't work the crime desk.

571
00:31:42,102 --> 00:31:43,605
Can you get us
to somebody that does?

572
00:31:43,738 --> 00:31:45,973
Yeah, sure.
Ask for Julie Trembley.

573
00:31:46,106 --> 00:31:48,275
T-R-E-M-B-L-E-Y.

574
00:31:48,409 --> 00:31:50,277
Let's talk about the bridge.

575
00:31:50,411 --> 00:31:51,613
What about the bridge?

576
00:31:51,746 --> 00:31:53,013
[CHUCKLES]

577
00:31:53,147 --> 00:31:55,215
Come on. He coulda
dropped the body anywhere,

578
00:31:55,349 --> 00:31:57,284
but he chose the busiest area.
Why?

579
00:31:57,418 --> 00:31:59,286
That's not cleanup.
That's a message.

580
00:31:59,420 --> 00:32:00,655
You're right.

581
00:32:00,789 --> 00:32:02,657
Zodiac,
Monster of Florence.

582
00:32:02,791 --> 00:32:05,359
Killers typically dump bodies
outside the city, a field--

583
00:32:05,492 --> 00:32:06,460
Or a park.

584
00:32:06,594 --> 00:32:07,896
Somewhere they'll go
unnoticed.

585
00:32:09,029 --> 00:32:11,198
So he wanted us to find her?

586
00:32:11,331 --> 00:32:13,967
The body getting stuck
in the rocks wasn't an accident.

587
00:32:14,101 --> 00:32:15,002
It was a choice.

588
00:32:17,037 --> 00:32:19,106
They did find a .357
at the riverside.

589
00:32:19,239 --> 00:32:21,208
Victim have any wounds
consistent with a gun?

590
00:32:21,341 --> 00:32:23,143
Oh, oh, where you going?
Library.

591
00:32:23,277 --> 00:32:24,546
Hey.

592
00:32:24,679 --> 00:32:25,547
Excuse me.

593
00:32:25,680 --> 00:32:26,714
Hey!

594
00:32:46,467 --> 00:32:48,803
[FLIPPING PAGES]

595
00:32:50,905 --> 00:32:52,306
This stuck with me.

596
00:32:52,439 --> 00:32:53,974
I just couldn't see
the connection until now.

597
00:32:54,107 --> 00:32:55,577
ALAN:
What are you talking about?

598
00:32:55,710 --> 00:32:56,945
"Remains of a unknown male,

599
00:32:57,077 --> 00:32:58,646
found after a structure fire."

600
00:32:58,780 --> 00:32:59,881
So?
High-heat fire.

601
00:33:00,013 --> 00:33:01,649
No prints,
no way to ID the body.

602
00:33:02,750 --> 00:33:04,117
One distinguishing factor.

603
00:33:04,251 --> 00:33:06,186
Teeth.
Like a fingerprint.

604
00:33:06,320 --> 00:33:07,589
Oh.

605
00:33:07,722 --> 00:33:09,624
What if it wasn't
torture or a fetish?

606
00:33:09,757 --> 00:33:11,425
What if he actually thought
we couldn't ID the body?

607
00:33:11,559 --> 00:33:13,293
But we did.

608
00:33:13,427 --> 00:33:15,697
We saw the face,
her-her-her dress.

609
00:33:15,830 --> 00:33:17,364
A mother could identify
the body.

610
00:33:19,132 --> 00:33:21,703
What if-- What if he
expected more decomp?

611
00:33:21,836 --> 00:33:24,873
He thought fish would eat away
at her features.

612
00:33:25,005 --> 00:33:27,007
That's-that's why he attracted
them with the fiber.

613
00:33:27,140 --> 00:33:28,342
Fibers?
Under the fingernails.

614
00:33:28,475 --> 00:33:30,512
The--
ALAN: Raw chicken. Okay.

615
00:33:30,645 --> 00:33:32,814
Who-who-who deals
with-with raw chicken

616
00:33:32,947 --> 00:33:34,147
on a daily basis?

617
00:33:34,281 --> 00:33:36,584
Cook.
Farmer.

618
00:33:36,718 --> 00:33:38,620
Butcher.
Butcher.

619
00:33:42,690 --> 00:33:43,958
[ALAN SIGHS]

620
00:33:50,732 --> 00:33:52,901
[KNOCKING AT DOOR]
Gleen.

621
00:33:53,033 --> 00:33:54,903
Is it locked?

622
00:33:55,035 --> 00:33:56,004
Says 9:00 a.m.

623
00:33:57,137 --> 00:33:58,338
[DOOR BELL JINGLES]

624
00:33:58,472 --> 00:33:59,641
AMELIA:
Mr. Gleen.

625
00:34:00,775 --> 00:34:01,876
Sheriff's department.

626
00:34:04,411 --> 00:34:07,080
[BIRDS CHIRPING]

627
00:34:07,214 --> 00:34:08,181
AMELIA:
You open?

628
00:34:15,590 --> 00:34:17,424
[DOORS OPEN]

629
00:34:17,559 --> 00:34:18,626
Can I help you?

630
00:34:22,730 --> 00:34:25,967
[RAIN PATTERING]

631
00:34:28,937 --> 00:34:30,638
It's almost midnight.
We need to get started

632
00:34:30,772 --> 00:34:32,439
before he realizes
he can call a lawyer.

633
00:34:32,574 --> 00:34:33,508
[DOOR OPENS]

634
00:34:36,143 --> 00:34:37,244
The results are negative.

635
00:34:37,377 --> 00:34:39,079
On what?
Gunpowder residue?

636
00:34:39,212 --> 00:34:40,347
On everything.

637
00:34:40,480 --> 00:34:42,182
Fingers, hands, elbows.

638
00:34:42,316 --> 00:34:43,885
No match.
Shit.

639
00:34:45,319 --> 00:34:46,588
ALAN:
You all right?

640
00:34:46,721 --> 00:34:48,823
You got a headache?
Mm.

641
00:34:48,957 --> 00:34:50,123
The worst kind.

642
00:34:50,257 --> 00:34:51,926
So who's going in first?

643
00:34:52,060 --> 00:34:54,227
Can't take turns.

644
00:34:54,361 --> 00:34:55,429
If that's our guy,
we gotta crack him

645
00:34:55,563 --> 00:34:56,496
on the first try.

646
00:34:56,631 --> 00:34:57,497
Crack him?

647
00:34:57,632 --> 00:34:59,132
It's been two days.

648
00:34:59,266 --> 00:35:01,803
We have too many leads,
no real suspects.

649
00:35:01,936 --> 00:35:02,971
Time is running out.

650
00:35:03,103 --> 00:35:04,639
We can't go in empty-handed.

651
00:35:04,772 --> 00:35:06,608
The butcher, where's his file?

652
00:35:06,741 --> 00:35:08,943
What file?

653
00:35:09,077 --> 00:35:11,613
Uh, all right.

654
00:35:13,447 --> 00:35:15,917
Here we go.

655
00:35:16,050 --> 00:35:18,820
Congratulations, you--
you just earned years' worth

656
00:35:18,953 --> 00:35:22,957
of evidence on your suspect,
enough to drag him to court.

657
00:35:23,091 --> 00:35:25,793
Let's crank the heat,
turn that room into a furnace.

658
00:35:25,927 --> 00:35:27,662
Nothing makes them talk faster.

659
00:35:27,795 --> 00:35:29,664
Have we considered
the possibility of a polygraph?

660
00:35:31,331 --> 00:35:33,467
HANK:
We do have a lie detector.

661
00:35:33,601 --> 00:35:35,168
ALAN:
If we can catch the killer,

662
00:35:35,302 --> 00:35:37,337
we find the missing girl.

663
00:35:43,243 --> 00:35:46,848
Okay. So we don't let him
leave until he takes the test.

664
00:35:46,981 --> 00:35:48,181
[CASE CLOSES]

665
00:35:48,315 --> 00:35:50,518
Whoa, whoa, whoa, whoa.

666
00:35:50,652 --> 00:35:52,620
We? Where do you think
you're going?

667
00:35:52,754 --> 00:35:54,622
In there with you.
No, you're not.

668
00:35:54,756 --> 00:35:56,223
I'm going in there alone.

669
00:35:56,356 --> 00:35:58,826
This guy has a history
of harassing girls.

670
00:35:58,960 --> 00:36:00,628
We need someone
with my perspective.

671
00:36:02,162 --> 00:36:03,497
And what perspective
would that be?

672
00:36:03,631 --> 00:36:04,532
That of a woman.

673
00:36:12,305 --> 00:36:13,273
[BEEPS]

674
00:36:14,809 --> 00:36:16,644
ALAN:
Thank you for waiting.

675
00:36:16,778 --> 00:36:18,846
Now, these are just
preliminary questions

676
00:36:18,980 --> 00:36:22,016
we're asking anybody that
we think can help with the case.

677
00:36:22,150 --> 00:36:23,483
Okay.
Okay.

678
00:36:23,618 --> 00:36:25,853
Before we get started,
can I get you anything?

679
00:36:25,987 --> 00:36:30,390
You want a coffee
or a cigarette, perhaps?

680
00:36:35,195 --> 00:36:36,097
We're new here.

681
00:36:37,165 --> 00:36:38,700
What about you?

682
00:36:38,833 --> 00:36:39,867
Live here long?

683
00:36:40,001 --> 00:36:41,501
BORIS:
I'm born and raised.

684
00:36:41,636 --> 00:36:43,838
You certainly know
your way around town.

685
00:36:43,971 --> 00:36:45,640
Local butcher.

686
00:36:45,773 --> 00:36:47,041
Everyone seems to trust you.

687
00:36:47,175 --> 00:36:48,643
I guess.
ALAN: Hm.

688
00:36:48,776 --> 00:36:50,310
Known the Otto girls long?

689
00:36:50,444 --> 00:36:52,245
Uh, a couple years.

690
00:36:52,379 --> 00:36:56,584
Um, Annie used to come in weekly
to pick up meat for her family.

691
00:36:56,718 --> 00:36:57,785
AMELIA:
Mm.

692
00:36:57,919 --> 00:36:59,754
Is that why you gave her
a summer job?

693
00:36:59,887 --> 00:37:01,288
Felt like you knew 'em?

694
00:37:01,421 --> 00:37:03,558
She seemed eager to work
and looked friendly,

695
00:37:03,691 --> 00:37:04,625
learned quickly.

696
00:37:05,893 --> 00:37:07,729
How long you been a butcher?

697
00:37:07,862 --> 00:37:09,496
30 years this summer.

698
00:37:09,630 --> 00:37:11,398
Wow, that's a long time.

699
00:37:11,532 --> 00:37:13,266
I bet the sight of blood
doesn't trigger you anymore.

700
00:37:13,400 --> 00:37:15,737
How-how old were you
when you started, you know,

701
00:37:15,870 --> 00:37:17,071
killing livestock?

702
00:37:18,405 --> 00:37:20,340
Five, six.

703
00:37:20,474 --> 00:37:21,709
Trained at the shop.

704
00:37:21,843 --> 00:37:23,678
AMELIA: Family business?
BORIS: Yeah.

705
00:37:23,811 --> 00:37:25,146
You own a gun, Mr. Gleen?

706
00:37:25,278 --> 00:37:27,081
BORIS:
I kept one at the house.

707
00:37:27,215 --> 00:37:29,150
Is that a requirement
for the job?

708
00:37:29,282 --> 00:37:30,885
No, more a passion
for hunting.

709
00:37:31,018 --> 00:37:31,953
[TAKES A DRAG]

710
00:37:32,086 --> 00:37:33,253
ALAN:
Fox?

711
00:37:33,386 --> 00:37:34,589
No, deer.

712
00:37:34,722 --> 00:37:36,389
Deer.

713
00:37:36,524 --> 00:37:38,425
And since when has that
been illegal?

714
00:37:38,559 --> 00:37:39,627
Your gun.

715
00:37:40,762 --> 00:37:42,130
Anything like this?

716
00:37:42,262 --> 00:37:43,598
BORIS:
Smith & Wesson.

717
00:37:43,731 --> 00:37:46,067
That's the most popular gun
in America, sir.

718
00:37:46,200 --> 00:37:47,902
So what?

719
00:37:48,035 --> 00:37:49,904
Strange choice
for hunting, though.

720
00:37:50,037 --> 00:37:52,707
Well, no, um, it's mostly
for target practice.

721
00:37:52,840 --> 00:37:54,075
ALAN:
So it is your gun?

722
00:37:54,208 --> 00:37:55,910
No, mine was stolen.

723
00:37:56,043 --> 00:37:58,079
ALAN: Stolen?
[TAKES A DRAG]

724
00:37:58,212 --> 00:37:59,680
I filed a report right away.

725
00:37:59,814 --> 00:38:01,149
Right.

726
00:38:01,281 --> 00:38:02,349
You mean this.

727
00:38:05,086 --> 00:38:06,521
Complaint of theft.

728
00:38:06,654 --> 00:38:09,389
So let me get this straight.

729
00:38:09,524 --> 00:38:12,927
Someone breaks into your house,
all they steal is a gun.

730
00:38:14,361 --> 00:38:16,564
Seems kinda odd.

731
00:38:16,697 --> 00:38:18,900
Well, I'm telling the truth.

732
00:38:19,033 --> 00:38:20,935
Can you turn the heat down
in here? God.

733
00:38:21,068 --> 00:38:23,070
Do you know the gun was found
at the crime scene, Mr. Gleen?

734
00:38:23,204 --> 00:38:24,337
[BORIS COUGHS]

735
00:38:24,471 --> 00:38:25,973
You guys, you guys--

736
00:38:26,107 --> 00:38:28,375
[COUGHS]

737
00:38:28,509 --> 00:38:30,545
Have me confused
with somebody else.

738
00:38:30,678 --> 00:38:32,113
What about your shop?

739
00:38:32,246 --> 00:38:35,850
It says here you had complaints
filed against you.

740
00:38:35,983 --> 00:38:37,218
Girls.

741
00:38:37,350 --> 00:38:39,554
Why do you have
so much information on me?

742
00:38:39,687 --> 00:38:40,988
Since when is that illegal?

743
00:38:42,455 --> 00:38:43,758
Oh, wait.

744
00:38:44,826 --> 00:38:46,661
You think I'm the killer.

745
00:38:46,794 --> 00:38:48,129
The day they went missing,

746
00:38:48,262 --> 00:38:50,298
the girls were seen
leaving their school.

747
00:38:50,430 --> 00:38:52,432
Told friends they were going
straight home.

748
00:38:52,567 --> 00:38:56,604
Now, you claim it's been months
since they were at your shop.

749
00:38:56,737 --> 00:38:58,139
Yeah, it's true.

750
00:38:58,272 --> 00:38:59,807
ALAN:
So how is it we found traces

751
00:38:59,941 --> 00:39:01,843
of raw chicken
under her fingernails?

752
00:39:03,511 --> 00:39:05,246
Same chicken
on your hands right now.

753
00:39:08,115 --> 00:39:09,717
Yeah, I'm not--
I'm not answering

754
00:39:09,851 --> 00:39:11,652
any more of your questions,
'cause I never

755
00:39:11,786 --> 00:39:13,621
touched those girls.

756
00:39:13,754 --> 00:39:15,590
You have me confused
with somebody else,

757
00:39:15,723 --> 00:39:17,558
so unless you have a real reason
to keep me here,

758
00:39:17,692 --> 00:39:19,459
I gotta get home
and tend to my mama.

759
00:39:29,136 --> 00:39:30,605
Thank you very much, Mr. Gleen.

760
00:39:30,738 --> 00:39:37,245
[♪♪♪]

761
00:39:37,377 --> 00:39:39,280
[DOOR OPENS, SHUTS]

762
00:39:39,412 --> 00:39:44,252
[TRAIN PASSING]

763
00:39:44,384 --> 00:39:46,419
AMELIA:
I'm telling you,
he's our guy.

764
00:39:46,554 --> 00:39:48,890
GRAYSMITH:
Now, how can you be sure?

765
00:39:49,023 --> 00:39:50,725
He's what they call
a "methodical killer."

766
00:39:50,858 --> 00:39:53,261
He's emotionally scarred,
shows signs

767
00:39:53,393 --> 00:39:54,862
of psychosexual
motivation.

768
00:39:54,996 --> 00:39:57,064
Come on, what signs?
HANK: Slow down.

769
00:39:57,198 --> 00:39:59,533
He practices on animals
before he attempts a kill.

770
00:39:59,667 --> 00:40:01,369
He fits the profile perfectly.

771
00:40:01,501 --> 00:40:03,304
We're basing this
on a theory?

772
00:40:03,436 --> 00:40:04,872
There's a difference
between killing chickens

773
00:40:05,006 --> 00:40:06,274
and murdering children.

774
00:40:06,406 --> 00:40:08,142
Sexually dysfunctional males
develop a hatred

775
00:40:08,276 --> 00:40:09,944
when they lack contact
with women their age.

776
00:40:10,077 --> 00:40:11,478
What are you saying?

777
00:40:11,612 --> 00:40:12,980
I'm saying
people get lonely.

778
00:40:13,114 --> 00:40:15,216
GRAYSMITH:
There were no traces
of sexual assault.

779
00:40:15,349 --> 00:40:17,585
Some achieve fulfillment
from the act of violence alone.

780
00:40:17,718 --> 00:40:18,920
Why?

781
00:40:19,053 --> 00:40:20,788
He hates women
because he wants them.

782
00:40:20,922 --> 00:40:22,556
GRAYSMITH:
But he can't bring
himself to have them.

783
00:40:22,690 --> 00:40:24,692
Which is why the body
doesn't show traces of rape.

784
00:40:24,825 --> 00:40:26,894
ALAN:
We need to focus less
on pre-crime behavior

785
00:40:27,028 --> 00:40:28,763
and more on post-crime
investigation.

786
00:40:28,896 --> 00:40:29,864
Meaning?

787
00:40:29,997 --> 00:40:32,133
Meaning less theory,
more proof.

788
00:40:32,266 --> 00:40:34,135
Just because you think
somebody's a killer doesn't mean

789
00:40:34,268 --> 00:40:35,736
they are one.

790
00:40:35,870 --> 00:40:37,437
Had you not let him go,
he'd still be hooked up

791
00:40:37,571 --> 00:40:39,106
to a lie detector
giving us answers.

792
00:40:39,240 --> 00:40:41,008
[INHALES] You know what--
[KNOCKING ON DOOR]

793
00:40:43,144 --> 00:40:45,079
Ah, the Otto files?

794
00:40:45,212 --> 00:40:46,147
Bring that in here.

795
00:40:51,385 --> 00:40:52,753
You-- you read the files.

796
00:40:52,887 --> 00:40:54,555
The guy has a history
of sexual harassment.

797
00:40:54,689 --> 00:40:56,257
Let's talk
to his former employees,

798
00:40:56,390 --> 00:40:58,326
they're all women,
and get one of them to testify.

799
00:40:58,458 --> 00:40:59,660
ALAN:
He's not our guy, Pembrock.

800
00:40:59,794 --> 00:41:00,928
Looks nothing
like the identikit.

801
00:41:01,062 --> 00:41:02,063
You said it was too dark to see.

802
00:41:02,196 --> 00:41:04,031
200 pounds, 6 foot.

803
00:41:04,165 --> 00:41:05,533
A guy like that's hard to miss.

804
00:41:05,666 --> 00:41:07,101
Brewster.
Yeah.

805
00:41:07,234 --> 00:41:09,270
It's a small town,
but it's not that small.

806
00:41:09,403 --> 00:41:10,671
You saw his hands.

807
00:41:10,805 --> 00:41:13,007
He could crush your face
with one blow,

808
00:41:13,140 --> 00:41:15,643
and he understands anatomy
for a living.

809
00:41:15,776 --> 00:41:17,778
You have to be strong
to pull something off like this,

810
00:41:17,912 --> 00:41:20,081
I give you that,
but this man is sick.

811
00:41:20,214 --> 00:41:22,016
He has diabetes.
I'm sorry.

812
00:41:22,149 --> 00:41:24,785
Why are we focusing all
of our energy on one guy?

813
00:41:24,919 --> 00:41:26,120
That's what I've been saying.

814
00:41:26,253 --> 00:41:27,487
Because now that
he knows he's a suspect,

815
00:41:27,621 --> 00:41:28,889
he'll destroy any remaining
evidence.

816
00:41:29,023 --> 00:41:30,458
Did you search the shop?
HANK: Not yet.

817
00:41:30,591 --> 00:41:32,760
There was hair on the floor.
Tell me we sampled it.

818
00:41:32,893 --> 00:41:35,463
With what warrant?

819
00:41:35,596 --> 00:41:39,567
Real-life applications
require real-life experience.

820
00:41:43,170 --> 00:41:45,239
[SLAMS BOOK]

821
00:41:45,373 --> 00:41:47,375
You can't always
judge a book by its cover,

822
00:41:47,508 --> 00:41:50,444
'cause sooner or later,
it'll bite you in the ass.

823
00:41:56,183 --> 00:41:59,887
[FOOTSTEPS RECEDING]

824
00:42:04,959 --> 00:42:09,096
[FOOTSTEPS APPROACHING]

825
00:42:29,150 --> 00:42:33,154
[FIRE BLAZING IN DISTANCE]

826
00:43:23,337 --> 00:43:25,239
I'm so sorry I'm late.

827
00:43:25,372 --> 00:43:27,108
I'm so sorry I'm late.

828
00:43:29,043 --> 00:43:32,880
[PANTING]

829
00:43:48,329 --> 00:43:50,297
[PULLS OFF TAPE]

830
00:43:50,431 --> 00:43:53,934
[PANTS]

831
00:44:00,875 --> 00:44:04,979
[FLIPPING PAGES]

832
00:45:05,172 --> 00:45:09,210
[INDISTINCT RADIO CHATTER]

833
00:45:09,343 --> 00:45:12,947
[POLICE SIREN WAILING]

834
00:45:35,302 --> 00:45:38,739
[POLICE SIREN WAILING]

835
00:45:38,872 --> 00:45:43,911
[♪♪♪]

836
00:45:57,891 --> 00:46:01,395
[FLICKS PAGES]

837
00:46:12,206 --> 00:46:13,907
[ELEVATOR BELL DINGS]

838
00:46:14,041 --> 00:46:15,075
Hi.

839
00:46:15,209 --> 00:46:16,110
Sorry.
Hey.

840
00:46:16,243 --> 00:46:18,445
Uh, is there a duplicate?

841
00:46:18,580 --> 00:46:20,414
In the archives, you mean?
Yeah.

842
00:46:20,548 --> 00:46:22,916
Nope. This is it.

843
00:46:23,050 --> 00:46:26,253
It's just that the checkout
list is incomplete.

844
00:46:26,387 --> 00:46:27,488
Oh, dear.

845
00:46:28,623 --> 00:46:29,624
Wonder what happened.

846
00:46:32,192 --> 00:46:34,729
Why would anyone
not want their name on here?

847
00:46:34,862 --> 00:46:35,896
[TAPS FINGER]

848
00:46:36,030 --> 00:46:40,834
[TRAFFIC PASSING]

849
00:46:40,968 --> 00:46:42,469
[ALAN CHUCKLES]

850
00:46:44,539 --> 00:46:47,441
Better at this than I was
when I was your age.

851
00:46:47,575 --> 00:46:48,842
Uh, I play during homeroom.

852
00:46:49,977 --> 00:46:50,978
[CHESS PIECE THUDS]

853
00:46:56,317 --> 00:46:57,652
ALAN:
You ever play her?

854
00:47:00,454 --> 00:47:01,756
Once.

855
00:47:01,889 --> 00:47:03,758
I don't think
she liked me.

856
00:47:03,891 --> 00:47:05,359
Your teachers say you
liked her.

857
00:47:07,928 --> 00:47:09,096
It's okay to have a crush.

858
00:47:09,229 --> 00:47:10,364
[CHUCKLES]

859
00:47:12,833 --> 00:47:15,135
I'm trying
to bring her sister home.

860
00:47:15,269 --> 00:47:16,870
You think you could help me?

861
00:47:21,609 --> 00:47:22,811
You recognize that?

862
00:47:26,180 --> 00:47:27,915
Yeah.

863
00:47:28,048 --> 00:47:30,685
Their mom said that she
didn't buy it for them.

864
00:47:30,819 --> 00:47:33,454
ALAN:
Gretta tell you
where she got it from?

865
00:47:33,588 --> 00:47:34,888
Was it a gift?

866
00:47:35,022 --> 00:47:36,524
It was?

867
00:47:37,792 --> 00:47:38,959
From someone older?

868
00:47:43,430 --> 00:47:44,965
Was it a friend?

869
00:47:45,099 --> 00:47:47,334
Maybe a secret friend?

870
00:47:47,468 --> 00:47:49,236
Do they teach chess
in police school?

871
00:47:51,205 --> 00:47:53,006
Do they teach chess
in police school?

872
00:47:53,140 --> 00:47:55,008
You know, they should.

873
00:47:55,142 --> 00:47:57,378
Learned more on this board
than I did out in the field.

874
00:47:57,512 --> 00:47:59,446
Isn't being a detective harder?

875
00:47:59,581 --> 00:48:00,749
Not really.

876
00:48:00,881 --> 00:48:03,551
Both about the big picture.

877
00:48:03,685 --> 00:48:07,388
Predicting moves,
anticipating outcomes.

878
00:48:07,522 --> 00:48:09,089
[CHESS PIECE THUDS]
LUKE: Check.

879
00:48:10,324 --> 00:48:11,726
It's okay.

880
00:48:11,860 --> 00:48:12,993
You can still win.

881
00:48:14,962 --> 00:48:17,431
HANK:
The Ottos are holding
a wake for their daughter.

882
00:48:17,565 --> 00:48:19,066
They're hoping
to raise awareness

883
00:48:19,199 --> 00:48:21,135
and remind people that
Anna's still missing.

884
00:48:21,268 --> 00:48:22,737
Where's Pembrock?

885
00:48:22,871 --> 00:48:24,271
Assumed she'd be with you.

886
00:48:31,746 --> 00:48:34,181
[CARS PASSING BY]

887
00:48:34,314 --> 00:48:40,220
[♪♪♪]

888
00:48:42,724 --> 00:48:48,596
[♪♪♪]

889
00:48:55,969 --> 00:48:58,272
Ms. Trembley?
Amelia Pembrock.

890
00:48:58,405 --> 00:49:01,141
Mike Bowers said you'd be
willing to talk about the case.

891
00:49:03,477 --> 00:49:04,746
Great.

892
00:49:04,879 --> 00:49:06,881
I was wondering
if I could buy you dinner.

893
00:49:09,183 --> 00:49:10,284
[CAR PASSING]

894
00:49:10,417 --> 00:49:12,620
[BIRDS CHIRPING]

895
00:49:12,754 --> 00:49:14,889
So how long did you work
at the butcher shop?

896
00:49:15,022 --> 00:49:16,323
Less than a year.

897
00:49:16,457 --> 00:49:19,059
I quit when things got
uncomfortable.

898
00:49:19,193 --> 00:49:20,227
He make passes at you?

899
00:49:20,360 --> 00:49:21,830
JULIE:
Flirting I'm used to.

900
00:49:21,962 --> 00:49:24,331
He was more interested
in proximity than contact,

901
00:49:24,465 --> 00:49:26,133
physical, that is.

902
00:49:26,266 --> 00:49:28,335
First, he asked me
to stay late nights,

903
00:49:28,469 --> 00:49:30,270
just watch me handle meat
from across the room,

904
00:49:30,404 --> 00:49:31,972
then the stalking began.

905
00:49:32,105 --> 00:49:34,274
Followed me home, got creepy.

906
00:49:34,408 --> 00:49:35,844
But he never touched you?

907
00:49:35,976 --> 00:49:37,779
Trust me, wouldn't let him.

908
00:49:37,912 --> 00:49:38,947
Asked him to stop.

909
00:49:39,079 --> 00:49:40,748
One night, I punched him.

910
00:49:40,882 --> 00:49:42,449
Holy shit.

911
00:49:42,584 --> 00:49:44,485
That-that only made
things worse.

912
00:49:44,619 --> 00:49:46,987
The more aggressive I got,
the more he was into it.

913
00:49:47,120 --> 00:49:49,624
Worst part is,
he never lost his cool.

914
00:49:49,757 --> 00:49:50,758
So then what happened?

915
00:49:50,892 --> 00:49:53,126
I quit, moved across town.

916
00:49:53,260 --> 00:49:55,062
Started working at the paper.

917
00:49:55,195 --> 00:49:57,331
Crime desk was always
my calling.

918
00:49:57,464 --> 00:49:59,333
You guys arrest people
with a warrant.

919
00:49:59,466 --> 00:50:01,736
All I need is to get 'em
on the front page.

920
00:50:01,870 --> 00:50:03,403
Did you ever see him again?

921
00:50:03,538 --> 00:50:05,339
JULIE:
He moved on
to his next employee.

922
00:50:05,472 --> 00:50:07,575
He prefers them young, you see.
Hmm.

923
00:50:07,709 --> 00:50:09,309
But to be honest,
I know the guy.

924
00:50:09,443 --> 00:50:10,979
He's not who you're looking for.

925
00:50:12,680 --> 00:50:14,147
What makes you say that?

926
00:50:14,281 --> 00:50:16,885
I spoke to Brewster,
saw the photos.

927
00:50:17,017 --> 00:50:18,720
I know what you think he did,

928
00:50:18,853 --> 00:50:21,523
but the Butcher,
he's not built for this.

929
00:50:21,656 --> 00:50:23,090
He's too shy.
Too shy?

930
00:50:23,223 --> 00:50:24,324
Too sloppy.

931
00:50:24,458 --> 00:50:26,528
Off the record,
I think there's a reason

932
00:50:26,661 --> 00:50:28,095
you're bathing in evidence.

933
00:50:28,228 --> 00:50:29,531
AMELIA:
Uh-huh.

934
00:50:29,664 --> 00:50:32,232
You guys found a .357
at the crime scene, right?

935
00:50:32,366 --> 00:50:34,769
And nobody found it
suspiciously easy?

936
00:50:34,903 --> 00:50:38,272
Four cases out of five,
murder weapon is never found,

937
00:50:38,405 --> 00:50:41,308
and you guys fish one out the
same morning less than 50 yards

938
00:50:41,441 --> 00:50:42,844
away from the body.

939
00:50:42,977 --> 00:50:45,145
How do you know all of this?

940
00:50:45,279 --> 00:50:48,716
I'd suck at my job
if I didn't have sources.

941
00:50:48,850 --> 00:50:52,085
When a crime this big
unfolds in a town this small,

942
00:50:52,219 --> 00:50:54,254
you can't expect people
to just stay put.

943
00:50:56,356 --> 00:50:57,725
Listen--
[SIGHS]

944
00:50:57,859 --> 00:50:59,794
I'm happy to help,
but if you want me

945
00:50:59,928 --> 00:51:03,063
to share my homework,
then you better do the same.

946
00:51:05,399 --> 00:51:06,734
[CAR PASSES]

947
00:51:06,868 --> 00:51:09,704
[TRAIN HORN BLARING IN DISTANCE]

948
00:51:09,837 --> 00:51:12,774
People dump stuff off the bridge
all the time, coins, garbage.

949
00:51:12,907 --> 00:51:14,341
So yeah--
Mm-hmm.

950
00:51:14,474 --> 00:51:16,010
You could assume the gun
was dumped on a different day

951
00:51:16,143 --> 00:51:17,277
by a different person

952
00:51:17,411 --> 00:51:19,614
for a different reason,
but look closely.

953
00:51:19,747 --> 00:51:21,381
Not a trace of rust.

954
00:51:21,516 --> 00:51:23,450
Half the serial number's
still legible.

955
00:51:23,585 --> 00:51:26,420
Which is how they pulled a clean
sample of the nitrate powder.

956
00:51:26,554 --> 00:51:28,890
Clues like these disappear
in a matter of hours.

957
00:51:29,023 --> 00:51:30,625
Somehow, this one didn't.

958
00:51:30,758 --> 00:51:32,627
How does everyone around here
know so much about guns?

959
00:51:32,760 --> 00:51:35,063
[CHUCKLES]
If you wanna speculate,
whoever it is,

960
00:51:35,195 --> 00:51:37,799
he's been carefully planting
evidence to throw you off.

961
00:51:37,932 --> 00:51:40,300
You're looking
in all the wrong places.

962
00:51:40,434 --> 00:51:42,369
This article you found.
Mm-hmm.

963
00:51:42,502 --> 00:51:44,404
You said Gretta's teeth
were smashed,

964
00:51:44,539 --> 00:51:47,041
her bite one of a kind.
So?

965
00:51:47,174 --> 00:51:50,110
So what if they didn't smash
them to cover up her identity?

966
00:51:50,243 --> 00:51:52,814
What if what they wanted to hide
was something else entirely?

967
00:51:52,947 --> 00:51:55,148
Like a mark?
A bite mark.

968
00:51:55,282 --> 00:51:58,452
Brookfield, June '94,
victim bites into the foot

969
00:51:58,586 --> 00:51:59,854
of her aggressor.

970
00:51:59,988 --> 00:52:02,255
Takes them years to notice
one of their suspects

971
00:52:02,389 --> 00:52:05,660
is hiding a scar, an exact match
to the victim's teeth.

972
00:52:05,793 --> 00:52:06,961
No teeth, no print.

973
00:52:07,095 --> 00:52:08,696
You'd have nothing
to ID it by.

974
00:52:11,164 --> 00:52:14,267
And all this evidence,
where'd you get it?

975
00:52:15,570 --> 00:52:16,504
Where did you get it?

976
00:52:16,638 --> 00:52:17,905
[LAUGHS]

977
00:52:19,272 --> 00:52:20,642
Okay.

978
00:52:20,775 --> 00:52:22,442
So let's say the butcher
was bitten.

979
00:52:22,577 --> 00:52:23,911
JULIE:
Uh, can I say something?

980
00:52:24,045 --> 00:52:25,980
Move on from the obvious.

981
00:52:26,114 --> 00:52:27,949
For years,
I let him call me names,

982
00:52:28,082 --> 00:52:29,884
haunt my social life.

983
00:52:30,018 --> 00:52:32,654
Nobody wants to see him punished
more than I do,

984
00:52:32,787 --> 00:52:34,689
but you're losing yourself
in a rabbit hole.

985
00:52:34,822 --> 00:52:37,391
Stop mistaking coincidence
for proof.

986
00:52:37,525 --> 00:52:38,993
First guy we interview,
and within hours,

987
00:52:39,127 --> 00:52:40,795
he cleans out his shop
and his home.

988
00:52:42,429 --> 00:52:44,766
I'm sure you're right,
no coincidence there.

989
00:52:48,002 --> 00:52:50,370
Thank you
for all your time.

990
00:52:55,743 --> 00:52:56,678
Appreciate it.

991
00:53:03,151 --> 00:53:06,353
[THUNDER RUMBLING]

992
00:53:07,855 --> 00:53:11,592
[FOOTSTEPS APPROACHING]

993
00:53:11,726 --> 00:53:13,193
Black looks good on you.

994
00:53:15,129 --> 00:53:16,864
You said
it's a wake, right?

995
00:53:16,998 --> 00:53:19,801
A memorial,
family organized.

996
00:53:21,102 --> 00:53:22,402
We say a few words then.

997
00:53:24,672 --> 00:53:26,874
Alan.
Hmm.

998
00:53:27,008 --> 00:53:29,409
It's been three days
since these girls disappeared,

999
00:53:31,079 --> 00:53:33,114
and I really appreciate you
coming here and working hard

1000
00:53:33,246 --> 00:53:34,849
on this case.

1001
00:53:34,982 --> 00:53:39,453
But you and I both know it,
if we don't find this girl

1002
00:53:39,587 --> 00:53:42,690
in the next 24 hours,
statistically speaking,

1003
00:53:42,824 --> 00:53:45,358
chances are she won't be back
at all.

1004
00:53:45,492 --> 00:53:46,393
Hmm.

1005
00:53:48,029 --> 00:53:50,064
You think the killer
will be there tonight,

1006
00:53:52,133 --> 00:53:53,333
out in the crowd?

1007
00:53:57,739 --> 00:53:59,774
[CROWS CAWING]

1008
00:53:59,907 --> 00:54:02,442
GRAYSMITH:
Tonight's about
a community uniting,

1009
00:54:02,577 --> 00:54:06,379
about the bigger plan
God has for us all.

1010
00:54:06,514 --> 00:54:09,784
Who loses their husband
is known as a widow.

1011
00:54:09,917 --> 00:54:13,955
Who loses their parents
is known as an orphan.

1012
00:54:14,088 --> 00:54:15,623
But if you lose a kid--

1013
00:54:15,757 --> 00:54:17,125
[SIGHS]

1014
00:54:17,290 --> 00:54:20,460
You don't even get a name
to be defined by.

1015
00:54:20,595 --> 00:54:23,030
Whose idea was it
to organize this memorial?

1016
00:54:23,164 --> 00:54:24,565
Mother? Father?

1017
00:54:24,699 --> 00:54:26,701
GRAYSMITH:
Should have to bury their child.

1018
00:54:26,834 --> 00:54:29,269
Neither. It was Pembrock's
suggestion.

1019
00:54:29,402 --> 00:54:32,039
Amelia. Why?

1020
00:54:32,173 --> 00:54:34,175
Because it wouldn't be uncommon
for him to take part,

1021
00:54:35,910 --> 00:54:37,745
participate in these kinds of--

1022
00:54:37,879 --> 00:54:41,015
Yes, community meetings.

1023
00:54:41,149 --> 00:54:43,117
You think he'd risk
exposing himself?

1024
00:54:43,251 --> 00:54:45,153
It's the next best thing
to going back

1025
00:54:45,285 --> 00:54:48,723
to the crime scene,
relive the experience,

1026
00:54:49,791 --> 00:54:51,626
some more of that euphoria.

1027
00:54:51,759 --> 00:54:53,227
We will find Anna.

1028
00:54:55,029 --> 00:54:57,999
We will bring her home.

1029
00:54:58,132 --> 00:55:01,669
These girls mean so much
to this community.

1030
00:55:01,803 --> 00:55:04,172
I understand the pain
of the parents,

1031
00:55:04,304 --> 00:55:09,510
'cause I myself would do
anything to protect my family.

1032
00:55:09,644 --> 00:55:11,879
And with God as my witness

1033
00:55:12,013 --> 00:55:16,551
I will not stop
until we bring our child home.

1034
00:55:16,684 --> 00:55:18,786
Where are the suspects?

1035
00:55:18,920 --> 00:55:20,822
We ask that you respect
the privacy of the family--

1036
00:55:20,955 --> 00:55:22,924
You were supposed
to protect them.

1037
00:55:23,057 --> 00:55:24,659
Give us names!

1038
00:55:24,792 --> 00:55:27,962
If you or anyone has information
that can help us find her--

1039
00:55:28,095 --> 00:55:29,764
Is Anna dead?

1040
00:55:29,897 --> 00:55:31,599
You're protecting the killer.
Say it!

1041
00:55:31,732 --> 00:55:33,433
[CROWD SHOUTING]

1042
00:55:33,568 --> 00:55:35,570
Wait, wait, wait, wait, wait.

1043
00:55:35,703 --> 00:55:37,071
MAN 3: Is Anna dead?
St-- stop.

1044
00:55:37,205 --> 00:55:39,307
All right, back, back, back,

1045
00:55:39,439 --> 00:55:40,473
back, back, back, back, go, go.

1046
00:55:40,608 --> 00:55:41,676
Take her!
Get her!

1047
00:55:45,947 --> 00:55:46,914
Mrs. Otto, I--
[SLAPS]

1048
00:55:47,048 --> 00:55:48,249
[CROWD GASPS]

1049
00:55:53,921 --> 00:55:56,824
I just found out the police
had a suspect in custody,

1050
00:55:56,958 --> 00:55:58,960
and you let them go.

1051
00:56:00,962 --> 00:56:07,400
When our daughters went missing,
we trusted you for your help.

1052
00:56:07,535 --> 00:56:09,537
To lead the investigation,
we allowed you

1053
00:56:09,670 --> 00:56:13,941
to follow dead ends off
the promise that you would be

1054
00:56:14,075 --> 00:56:15,877
delivering justice.
Mr. Otto--

1055
00:56:16,010 --> 00:56:19,180
But now my daughter's future
could end up being

1056
00:56:19,313 --> 00:56:22,382
yesterday's headlines.

1057
00:56:22,516 --> 00:56:26,520
And we will not find peace
until our Anna is brought back.

1058
00:56:28,656 --> 00:56:29,690
Now,

1059
00:56:32,492 --> 00:56:36,030
whether Anna's alive

1060
00:56:36,163 --> 00:56:39,100
or in a body bag
is entirely up to you.

1061
00:56:42,169 --> 00:56:43,638
Come on. Let's go.

1062
00:56:48,743 --> 00:56:49,944
[ILSA SNIFFLES]

1063
00:56:50,077 --> 00:56:51,746
[INDISTINCT CHATTER]

1064
00:56:56,884 --> 00:56:59,620
[SIGHS]
This is getting out of hand.

1065
00:56:59,754 --> 00:57:01,622
The city's turning on us.
They want an outcome.

1066
00:57:01,756 --> 00:57:03,391
They want closure.

1067
00:57:03,524 --> 00:57:06,394
Well, unsolved murders is
bad press for anyone involved.

1068
00:57:06,527 --> 00:57:08,195
We're running out
of options.

1069
00:57:08,329 --> 00:57:10,831
Sometimes any suspect
is better than no suspect.

1070
00:57:10,965 --> 00:57:14,568
How can a case this
simple be so difficult to solve?

1071
00:57:14,702 --> 00:57:17,171
Maybe it's not as simple
as we think.

1072
00:57:17,305 --> 00:57:18,839
We've gotta be missing
something.

1073
00:57:21,208 --> 00:57:23,443
Okay,
the Otto girls leave school here

1074
00:57:25,246 --> 00:57:26,914
and they're meant
to arrive home here.

1075
00:57:28,082 --> 00:57:29,717
But they never do.

1076
00:57:29,850 --> 00:57:32,420
Which means somewhere
along this path our suspect

1077
00:57:32,553 --> 00:57:33,821
approaches them.

1078
00:57:33,955 --> 00:57:35,990
Someone they're
familiar with,

1079
00:57:36,123 --> 00:57:38,092
not out of the ordinary.

1080
00:57:38,225 --> 00:57:39,927
AMELIA:
They trust him.

1081
00:57:40,061 --> 00:57:41,862
Maybe he offered 'em
a ride home,

1082
00:57:43,731 --> 00:57:46,466
gave 'em false information,
tricked them into following him.

1083
00:57:46,600 --> 00:57:48,501
How do we know it was
a planned homicide

1084
00:57:48,636 --> 00:57:49,971
and not a kidnapping?

1085
00:57:50,104 --> 00:57:51,038
We don't.

1086
00:57:52,573 --> 00:57:55,009
Maybe all he wanted
to do was play.

1087
00:57:55,142 --> 00:57:56,410
GRAYSMITH:
Hmm?

1088
00:57:56,544 --> 00:57:58,145
In the hopes to let 'em go,

1089
00:57:58,279 --> 00:58:00,147
promise
to make it his little game.

1090
00:58:00,281 --> 00:58:02,450
He gets pleasure
from the proximity alone.

1091
00:58:02,583 --> 00:58:04,852
Game?
What kind of game?

1092
00:58:04,986 --> 00:58:06,454
AMELIA:
Could be anyone's guess.

1093
00:58:06,587 --> 00:58:08,956
Soft choking,
dominating the victim.

1094
00:58:09,090 --> 00:58:10,725
ALAN:
So he ties 'em up.

1095
00:58:10,858 --> 00:58:13,361
It's all part of the agreement.

1096
00:58:13,493 --> 00:58:18,299
Except they panic,
things get out of hand

1097
00:58:18,432 --> 00:58:20,868
real quick,
and the game turns into a fight.

1098
00:58:21,002 --> 00:58:22,336
A fight into murder.

1099
00:58:22,470 --> 00:58:24,305
So Gretta tries
to defend herself.

1100
00:58:24,438 --> 00:58:25,706
She fights back.

1101
00:58:26,841 --> 00:58:29,010
She bites him.

1102
00:58:29,143 --> 00:58:33,014
That wound is now
a one-of-a-kind fingerprint.

1103
00:58:33,147 --> 00:58:35,182
So he smashes the teeth--
AMELIA: Mm-hmm.

1104
00:58:35,316 --> 00:58:37,051
So it won't come back to him.

1105
00:58:37,184 --> 00:58:38,786
When it comes time
to ditch the body,

1106
00:58:38,919 --> 00:58:41,689
he chooses a place where it's
likely to get stuck underwater.

1107
00:58:41,822 --> 00:58:43,557
Trying to wash away
all the evidence.

1108
00:58:43,691 --> 00:58:45,559
ALAN:
But he doesn't consider
the tide.

1109
00:58:45,693 --> 00:58:47,762
The body surfaces too soon.

1110
00:58:47,895 --> 00:58:49,764
We find it.

1111
00:58:49,897 --> 00:58:52,666
After he kills the first twin,
he doesn't let the other one go.

1112
00:58:52,800 --> 00:58:54,702
GRAYSMITH:
Why?

1113
00:58:54,835 --> 00:58:56,370
Well,
he can't just drop her off.

1114
00:58:56,505 --> 00:58:57,571
She'll talk.

1115
00:58:57,705 --> 00:58:59,173
ALAN:
Right.

1116
00:58:59,306 --> 00:59:00,741
So he detains her.

1117
00:59:02,443 --> 00:59:04,513
He's hoping to keep playing.

1118
00:59:04,645 --> 00:59:06,881
Same game, different outcome.

1119
00:59:08,716 --> 00:59:09,917
All right.

1120
00:59:10,051 --> 00:59:12,553
Let's just say this
is what happens.

1121
00:59:12,686 --> 00:59:13,687
All right?

1122
00:59:14,889 --> 00:59:16,724
Now the question is who.

1123
00:59:16,857 --> 00:59:18,592
AMELIA:
I went back to the library,

1124
00:59:18,726 --> 00:59:20,294
found a ledger
on child abduction.

1125
00:59:21,362 --> 00:59:22,696
The checkout list was ripped.

1126
00:59:22,830 --> 00:59:24,031
One name was missing.

1127
00:59:24,165 --> 00:59:25,399
GRAYSMITH:
Who wouldn't want a record

1128
00:59:25,534 --> 00:59:26,567
of ever checking out
that binder?

1129
00:59:26,700 --> 00:59:27,635
I know who.

1130
00:59:30,037 --> 00:59:32,440
The Otto girls left
for school here.

1131
00:59:32,573 --> 00:59:35,544
The butcher lives
with his mother here.

1132
00:59:35,676 --> 00:59:39,046
Curb to curb, it's a block.

1133
00:59:39,180 --> 00:59:43,350
He watched them out his window
for months before finally doing

1134
00:59:43,484 --> 00:59:44,618
something about it.

1135
00:59:45,953 --> 00:59:48,322
Did you know about that?

1136
00:59:48,456 --> 00:59:50,458
He didn't take them
to his shop.

1137
00:59:50,624 --> 00:59:51,759
He took them
to his home.

1138
00:59:55,029 --> 00:59:56,797
Ah, it's a great theory.

1139
00:59:59,200 --> 01:00:00,734
Still doesn't explain the gun.

1140
01:00:07,341 --> 01:00:08,976
He's right.

1141
01:00:09,110 --> 01:00:11,112
No bullets fired,
no shells recovered,

1142
01:00:11,245 --> 01:00:12,646
so how does that factor in?

1143
01:00:12,780 --> 01:00:14,482
Maybe it doesn't.

1144
01:00:14,615 --> 01:00:15,550
Maybe it never did.

1145
01:00:17,251 --> 01:00:19,420
We have to get a warrant
to search the butcher's home.

1146
01:00:19,554 --> 01:00:21,255
Now, how am I gonna get
the prosecutor

1147
01:00:21,388 --> 01:00:23,824
to give us a warrant when we
barely have any evidence?

1148
01:00:23,958 --> 01:00:26,293
Every day that goes by,
it gets more and more difficult

1149
01:00:26,427 --> 01:00:28,028
to collect any.
We're trying.

1150
01:00:28,162 --> 01:00:29,096
AMELIA:
Aren't you sick of trying?

1151
01:00:29,230 --> 01:00:30,631
Stop.
AMELIA: It's either this

1152
01:00:30,764 --> 01:00:32,666
or we just wait
for the other body to wash up.

1153
01:00:32,800 --> 01:00:33,968
We know!

1154
01:00:34,101 --> 01:00:35,269
Do you really think
this is a coincidence?

1155
01:00:35,402 --> 01:00:36,303
[BANGS TABLE]
Enough!

1156
01:00:39,206 --> 01:00:43,277
Look, you came here to assist
and observe.

1157
01:00:45,580 --> 01:00:47,214
But so far all I've seen
from you is guesses

1158
01:00:47,348 --> 01:00:48,382
and theories.

1159
01:00:50,284 --> 01:00:52,686
You've been absent
for days assembling a case

1160
01:00:52,820 --> 01:00:53,888
of your own.

1161
01:00:54,021 --> 01:00:56,123
What are you really
trying to say?

1162
01:00:56,257 --> 01:00:57,626
You're good
at figuring people out.

1163
01:00:57,791 --> 01:00:58,792
Why don't you tell me?

1164
01:01:00,928 --> 01:01:02,196
You want honesty?

1165
01:01:02,329 --> 01:01:03,297
You're lost.

1166
01:01:05,266 --> 01:01:06,934
A caricature
of your former self,

1167
01:01:08,469 --> 01:01:11,272
desperate to retire
in spite of your age.

1168
01:01:11,405 --> 01:01:13,974
You reek of alcohol
every day that we're out here.

1169
01:01:15,743 --> 01:01:17,211
At this point,
you're really only as good as

1170
01:01:17,344 --> 01:01:19,747
your opinions,
which you're reticent to offer,

1171
01:01:19,880 --> 01:01:21,015
because you have none.

1172
01:01:24,653 --> 01:01:26,720
The only good part of your day

1173
01:01:28,489 --> 01:01:30,257
is the few seconds
after you wake up,

1174
01:01:32,661 --> 01:01:34,161
before you remember
that you're waking up

1175
01:01:34,295 --> 01:01:35,630
to a world that he's not in.

1176
01:01:44,305 --> 01:01:45,472
ALAN:
Go home, Amelia.

1177
01:01:51,513 --> 01:01:52,614
Get some sleep.

1178
01:01:54,649 --> 01:01:57,251
[FOOTSTEPS RECEDING]

1179
01:01:57,384 --> 01:01:58,285
GRAYSMITH:
Amelia.

1180
01:02:02,089 --> 01:02:05,594
Maybe you're onto something,
but without enough evidence,

1181
01:02:05,726 --> 01:02:08,262
even the best of us
couldn't draw a connection.

1182
01:02:10,164 --> 01:02:12,166
You think he really did it?

1183
01:02:12,299 --> 01:02:13,200
Prove it.

1184
01:02:13,334 --> 01:02:16,103
[THUNDER RUMBLING]

1185
01:02:16,237 --> 01:02:18,607
[FOOTSTEPS RECEDING]

1186
01:02:18,739 --> 01:02:24,878
[♪♪♪]

1187
01:02:29,618 --> 01:02:32,152
[DOG BARKING]

1188
01:02:36,890 --> 01:02:38,259
[CLICKS LIGHTER]

1189
01:02:42,363 --> 01:02:45,600
[CAR SCREECHING IN DISTANCE]

1190
01:02:53,807 --> 01:02:55,610
[DOG BARKING]

1191
01:03:14,696 --> 01:03:18,667
[POLICE SIREN WAILING]

1192
01:03:18,799 --> 01:03:24,706
[♪♪♪]

1193
01:03:44,858 --> 01:03:50,699
[♪♪♪]

1194
01:03:59,674 --> 01:04:00,874
[WINDOW SLIDES OPEN]

1195
01:04:08,282 --> 01:04:12,721
[DOOR CREAKS OPEN]

1196
01:04:12,853 --> 01:04:15,222
[HISSING FROM TANK]

1197
01:04:34,642 --> 01:04:38,412
[HISSING FROM TANK]

1198
01:04:55,129 --> 01:04:58,198
[PLASTIC RUSTLES]

1199
01:05:08,777 --> 01:05:10,244
[FLY BUZZING]
[GRUNTS]

1200
01:05:20,320 --> 01:05:24,358
[CLANKING OPEN, CLOSES]

1201
01:05:37,605 --> 01:05:38,740
[CLANKS CLOSED]

1202
01:05:42,109 --> 01:05:43,043
[DRAWER THUDS]

1203
01:06:05,466 --> 01:06:06,366
[DOOR CLOSES]

1204
01:06:12,105 --> 01:06:16,443
[EXHALES]
[KEYS JANGLE]

1205
01:06:23,885 --> 01:06:24,819
[GASPS]

1206
01:06:34,361 --> 01:06:35,395
Mama?

1207
01:06:36,531 --> 01:06:38,332
[MOTHER WHIMPERS]

1208
01:06:38,465 --> 01:06:39,399
Mama.

1209
01:06:39,534 --> 01:06:42,135
[MOTHER WHIMPERING]

1210
01:06:49,844 --> 01:06:51,980
[THUDS]

1211
01:06:58,285 --> 01:07:02,389
[PANTING]

1212
01:07:09,531 --> 01:07:10,430
[UNZIPS BAG]

1213
01:07:11,799 --> 01:07:12,667
[PLASTIC RUSTLES]

1214
01:07:12,800 --> 01:07:14,869
[SIGHS]
Oh God.

1215
01:07:21,609 --> 01:07:24,979
[WINDOW CREAKS CLOSED]

1216
01:07:26,714 --> 01:07:31,051
[THUDS]
[PANTING]

1217
01:07:35,657 --> 01:07:39,159
[FOOTSTEPS APPROACHING]

1218
01:07:39,293 --> 01:07:42,764
[INDISTINCT CHATTER]

1219
01:07:44,832 --> 01:07:47,401
GRAYSMITH:
Just in time.
That's the prosecutor's office.

1220
01:07:47,535 --> 01:07:49,671
We can have a warrant
as early as today.

1221
01:07:50,705 --> 01:07:51,873
What's this?

1222
01:07:52,006 --> 01:07:53,173
GRAYSMITH:
New evidence.

1223
01:07:53,307 --> 01:07:54,709
Matches the girl's shoes.

1224
01:07:54,842 --> 01:07:56,878
Where'd you get it?
They didn't say.

1225
01:07:57,011 --> 01:08:01,281
Came in middle of the night,
anonymous drop-off.

1226
01:08:01,415 --> 01:08:06,119
More importantly, the DNA
on it matches the butcher.

1227
01:08:06,253 --> 01:08:09,924
When we found the girl,
Gretta was wearing both shoes.

1228
01:08:10,058 --> 01:08:11,491
Did the family call in
a missing shoe?

1229
01:08:11,626 --> 01:08:12,627
No.

1230
01:08:12,760 --> 01:08:14,494
Is her DNA on the shoe?

1231
01:08:14,629 --> 01:08:15,663
GRAYSMITH:
Technically, no.

1232
01:08:15,797 --> 01:08:17,464
The size and color are a match.

1233
01:08:18,600 --> 01:08:20,635
This proves nothing.

1234
01:08:20,768 --> 01:08:22,369
We can't go around
arresting people

1235
01:08:22,502 --> 01:08:24,204
for their shopping habits.

1236
01:08:24,338 --> 01:08:26,774
AMELIA:
The butcher doesn't
have kids, no nieces either.

1237
01:08:26,908 --> 01:08:29,376
What is a grown man doing buying
children's shoes?

1238
01:08:29,510 --> 01:08:31,244
This isn't proof.

1239
01:08:31,378 --> 01:08:33,715
ALAN:
You've got to be pretty
desperate to call this evidence.

1240
01:08:33,848 --> 01:08:35,315
Well, we are desperate.

1241
01:08:35,449 --> 01:08:38,653
Guilty or not, you can't
take back a murder charge.

1242
01:08:38,786 --> 01:08:40,088
People will start talking.

1243
01:08:40,220 --> 01:08:41,288
We certainly wouldn't
want them to come

1244
01:08:41,421 --> 01:08:42,757
to the wrong conclusion,
would we?

1245
01:08:45,325 --> 01:08:47,127
You're making a mistake.

1246
01:08:47,260 --> 01:08:48,863
GRAYSMITH:
Look, if we want a warrant,

1247
01:08:48,997 --> 01:08:50,965
I need to make a call
in the next five minutes

1248
01:08:51,099 --> 01:08:52,867
or we have to wait till Monday.

1249
01:08:53,001 --> 01:08:54,002
Where are you going?

1250
01:08:57,404 --> 01:08:59,607
I'm going to eat,
to sleep,

1251
01:08:59,741 --> 01:09:01,943
maybe relax
a little bit.

1252
01:09:02,076 --> 01:09:04,512
You know,
you should do the same.

1253
01:09:04,646 --> 01:09:06,981
You're not good
to anybody being up all night.

1254
01:09:07,115 --> 01:09:08,315
Kramer--
And uh,

1255
01:09:08,448 --> 01:09:10,852
Ripley's on duty
downstairs.

1256
01:09:10,985 --> 01:09:12,820
You can get that wound
looked at.

1257
01:09:12,954 --> 01:09:17,091
[TRAIN PASSING IN BACKGROUND]

1258
01:09:21,428 --> 01:09:23,497
[SNIPS]

1259
01:09:23,631 --> 01:09:24,766
Done.

1260
01:09:26,000 --> 01:09:27,101
Guess that glass of wine
in the bath

1261
01:09:27,234 --> 01:09:28,335
wasn't such a great idea.

1262
01:09:28,468 --> 01:09:29,704
[CHUCKLES]

1263
01:09:29,837 --> 01:09:30,872
Wine glass, huh?

1264
01:09:32,507 --> 01:09:34,341
'Cause judging by that cut
I just stitched up,

1265
01:09:34,474 --> 01:09:37,011
I'd say you were drinking out
of a 5-foot metal vase.

1266
01:09:39,179 --> 01:09:40,148
[PEN CLICKS]

1267
01:09:40,280 --> 01:09:41,481
You'll report it?

1268
01:09:41,616 --> 01:09:42,850
I have to.

1269
01:09:42,984 --> 01:09:44,484
Lab stocks up on my supplies.

1270
01:09:44,619 --> 01:09:47,254
They're gonna need
to know what I used it for.

1271
01:09:47,387 --> 01:09:48,690
Why?

1272
01:09:48,823 --> 01:09:50,591
What do you think
I should write?

1273
01:09:52,160 --> 01:09:53,661
A minor domestic accident.

1274
01:09:55,495 --> 01:09:58,166
Whatever it takes
to keep you out in the field.

1275
01:09:58,298 --> 01:10:00,735
Now, get out there
and catch that son of a bitch.

1276
01:10:00,868 --> 01:10:01,769
AMELIA:
Thank you.

1277
01:10:03,805 --> 01:10:08,142
[CAR PASSES BY]

1278
01:10:10,511 --> 01:10:16,383
[♪♪♪]

1279
01:10:17,952 --> 01:10:19,319
A whiskey, double.

1280
01:10:19,453 --> 01:10:20,588
Please.

1281
01:10:23,323 --> 01:10:27,095
[BACKGROUND CHATTER, LAUGHTER]

1282
01:10:31,566 --> 01:10:35,770
[FOOTSTEPS APPROACHING]

1283
01:10:40,508 --> 01:10:41,876
I should thank you.

1284
01:10:44,078 --> 01:10:47,481
With a single accusation,
you managed to shake

1285
01:10:47,615 --> 01:10:50,383
my life up with ruthless
efficiency.

1286
01:10:53,521 --> 01:10:58,726
First, you put my name
in the papers.

1287
01:11:01,095 --> 01:11:06,834
Then within a couple days, all
my customers just disappeared.

1288
01:11:08,703 --> 01:11:10,905
That's when the death threats
started.

1289
01:11:11,038 --> 01:11:13,273
[LAUGHTER IN BACKGROUND]

1290
01:11:14,675 --> 01:11:16,144
I had to close the shop.

1291
01:11:19,080 --> 01:11:22,717
Five generations of work, gone.

1292
01:11:22,850 --> 01:11:26,286
[CHEERING AND CLAPPING
IN BACKGROUND]

1293
01:11:26,419 --> 01:11:27,989
God only knows how I'm gonna
care for mama.

1294
01:11:29,422 --> 01:11:31,092
But-- but--

1295
01:11:31,225 --> 01:11:32,860
[SIGHS]

1296
01:11:32,994 --> 01:11:37,165
But at least we know
how one man's

1297
01:11:37,297 --> 01:11:43,204
opinion can thoroughly destroy
the life of a complete stranger.

1298
01:11:45,673 --> 01:11:49,177
But that day you came to see me,
I guess you didn't

1299
01:11:49,309 --> 01:11:51,279
consider that, did you?

1300
01:11:51,411 --> 01:11:52,479
[COUGHS]

1301
01:11:52,613 --> 01:11:54,314
[SNIFFLES]

1302
01:11:54,447 --> 01:11:56,050
[SIPS, GULPS]

1303
01:11:56,184 --> 01:11:57,317
You know detective,

1304
01:11:59,520 --> 01:12:02,023
I have nothing to hide from you.

1305
01:12:02,156 --> 01:12:03,224
[LAUGHS]

1306
01:12:03,356 --> 01:12:04,692
But-- [SNIFFLES]

1307
01:12:04,826 --> 01:12:08,696
But even if I did,
what makes you think

1308
01:12:08,830 --> 01:12:10,531
I'd ever tell you?

1309
01:12:29,116 --> 01:12:30,218
[GULPS]

1310
01:12:32,620 --> 01:12:33,521
[SIGHS]

1311
01:12:40,094 --> 01:12:44,332
[POLICE SIREN WAILING]

1312
01:12:50,671 --> 01:12:52,439
[TIRES SCREECHING]

1313
01:12:52,573 --> 01:12:54,242
WOMAN 2:
What's going on?

1314
01:12:54,374 --> 01:12:56,043
[WHISTLE BLOWING]

1315
01:12:59,180 --> 01:13:00,882
[DOOR OPENS, BELL JINGLES]

1316
01:13:01,015 --> 01:13:03,517
Amelia, it's over.

1317
01:13:04,986 --> 01:13:07,454
The butcher is-- is dead.

1318
01:13:07,588 --> 01:13:09,123
[PANTS]

1319
01:13:11,192 --> 01:13:14,829
<i>We all knew this case would</i>
<i>reach a satisfying conclusion.</i>

1320
01:13:16,898 --> 01:13:20,334
Earlier today,
Boris Gleen took his own life.

1321
01:13:22,203 --> 01:13:25,573
Though the confession
wasn't secured in time,

1322
01:13:25,706 --> 01:13:28,276
we have reason
to believe he was the man

1323
01:13:28,408 --> 01:13:31,712
who kidnapped
and murdered Gretta Otto.

1324
01:13:31,846 --> 01:13:33,281
So what was his motive?

1325
01:13:33,413 --> 01:13:34,782
Well, we have yet to--

1326
01:13:34,916 --> 01:13:36,284
Is it true that
one of the investigators

1327
01:13:36,416 --> 01:13:37,518
was seen drinking
with the suspect?

1328
01:13:37,652 --> 01:13:38,686
Wha-- uh,
I'm not sure about that.

1329
01:13:38,819 --> 01:13:40,054
What about Anna Otto?

1330
01:13:40,187 --> 01:13:41,689
REPORTER:
Have they found a body?

1331
01:13:41,822 --> 01:13:43,224
No, we have not yet
found a body. But we are--

1332
01:13:43,357 --> 01:13:44,892
[CROWD CLAMORING]

1333
01:13:45,026 --> 01:13:49,664
Please, the investigation
will continue as planned.

1334
01:13:49,797 --> 01:13:52,099
Justice for Gretta
has been made!

1335
01:13:52,233 --> 01:13:55,102
[CROWD SHOUTING]

1336
01:14:08,950 --> 01:14:11,451
ALAN:
Congratulations.
That's a hell of a first case.

1337
01:14:13,621 --> 01:14:14,755
Must be happy.

1338
01:14:16,424 --> 01:14:19,126
Methodical theory,
predictable outcome,

1339
01:14:20,695 --> 01:14:22,430
everything by the books.

1340
01:14:22,563 --> 01:14:24,799
That's what you wanted,
right?

1341
01:14:24,932 --> 01:14:26,067
[SCOFFS]

1342
01:14:32,573 --> 01:14:35,242
I'm packing my bags,
leaving tomorrow,

1343
01:14:35,376 --> 01:14:36,277
first flight out.

1344
01:14:37,378 --> 01:14:38,846
You should probably do the same.

1345
01:14:38,980 --> 01:14:42,984
[THUNDER RUMBLING]

1346
01:14:47,655 --> 01:14:50,057
They still teach forensic
mythology at the academy?

1347
01:14:52,159 --> 01:14:53,127
Mm-hmm.

1348
01:14:55,196 --> 01:14:57,698
My first week,
they gathered all the cadets

1349
01:14:57,832 --> 01:15:00,134
this big auditorium,

1350
01:15:00,267 --> 01:15:02,236
wanted to speak to us
about good and evil.

1351
01:15:05,373 --> 01:15:07,742
Saint Gabriel and the dragon,
you know that story?

1352
01:15:08,809 --> 01:15:09,910
No.

1353
01:15:10,044 --> 01:15:11,979
[CLICKS TONGUE]

1354
01:15:12,113 --> 01:15:13,914
There was this knight
named Gabriel.

1355
01:15:15,616 --> 01:15:17,018
They said he was a saint,
but really he was just

1356
01:15:17,151 --> 01:15:18,152
a good man.

1357
01:15:20,454 --> 01:15:22,790
Sword, code of honor.

1358
01:15:24,859 --> 01:15:28,462
And one day this dragon shows up
and so he travels

1359
01:15:28,596 --> 01:15:29,964
to the mountains to fight it.

1360
01:15:32,166 --> 01:15:33,567
But it's not the type
of dragon you see

1361
01:15:33,701 --> 01:15:36,037
in fairy tales,
it's something much worse.

1362
01:15:38,005 --> 01:15:41,809
Doesn't just breathe fire,
it-- it whispers lies,

1363
01:15:41,942 --> 01:15:43,844
it stirs fear.

1364
01:15:43,978 --> 01:15:48,849
It turns people on each other,
eats them from within.

1365
01:15:51,819 --> 01:15:54,021
So days go by and then weeks,
and everybody's waiting

1366
01:15:54,155 --> 01:15:55,756
for Gabriel to come back, but

1367
01:15:58,893 --> 01:15:59,860
never does.

1368
01:16:02,863 --> 01:16:04,298
After all these years
in the academy,

1369
01:16:04,432 --> 01:16:06,200
that's the first thing you hear,

1370
01:16:08,669 --> 01:16:10,371
but it's the hardest thing
to learn,

1371
01:16:13,407 --> 01:16:15,042
is that sometimes
the dragon wins.

1372
01:16:22,650 --> 01:16:24,885
I just wanted
to catch him alive,

1373
01:16:25,019 --> 01:16:26,020
you know?

1374
01:16:38,065 --> 01:16:42,736
Retirement won't bring him back.

1375
01:16:46,874 --> 01:16:48,375
I know it was never about that.

1376
01:16:54,381 --> 01:16:56,117
You just want the feeling
to go away.

1377
01:17:00,488 --> 01:17:01,655
You want closure.

1378
01:17:03,457 --> 01:17:06,427
[THUNDER RUMBLES]

1379
01:17:11,899 --> 01:17:12,800
[DOOR CREAKS OPEN]

1380
01:17:12,933 --> 01:17:13,934
ALAN:
Hello?

1381
01:17:15,570 --> 01:17:16,605
Hey.

1382
01:17:16,737 --> 01:17:17,606
COLIN:
Hey.

1383
01:17:17,738 --> 01:17:18,973
Building's empty.

1384
01:17:19,106 --> 01:17:21,041
Didn't think I'd find you here.

1385
01:17:21,175 --> 01:17:23,177
Come to say goodbye?

1386
01:17:23,310 --> 01:17:25,746
Yeah, I'm
on the first flight out.

1387
01:17:25,880 --> 01:17:28,682
I wanted to say thank you
for everything.

1388
01:17:28,816 --> 01:17:30,317
You tried your best.

1389
01:17:30,451 --> 01:17:31,685
We all did.
Yeah.

1390
01:17:31,819 --> 01:17:33,754
I don't know, maybe this is
all my fault.

1391
01:17:35,322 --> 01:17:36,423
What's that?

1392
01:17:37,892 --> 01:17:39,594
It's a mold
of the girl's teeth.

1393
01:17:39,760 --> 01:17:41,795
Finally came in.

1394
01:17:41,929 --> 01:17:45,032
Who knew reconstruction
could take this long?

1395
01:17:45,166 --> 01:17:47,034
I doubt it would've made
a difference.

1396
01:17:47,168 --> 01:17:48,335
Want me
to get it off your hands?

1397
01:17:48,469 --> 01:17:49,638
You checked the butcher's
body, right?

1398
01:17:49,770 --> 01:17:50,804
Yeah.
For bite marks?

1399
01:17:50,938 --> 01:17:52,039
I could add it
to the inventory

1400
01:17:52,173 --> 01:17:53,340
with the rest of the evidence.

1401
01:17:53,474 --> 01:17:55,209
Let me take it.
No, I-I can handle it.

1402
01:17:55,342 --> 01:17:56,310
COLIN:
All right.

1403
01:17:57,512 --> 01:17:58,412
All right.
Yeah.

1404
01:17:58,547 --> 01:18:00,181
Thanks, man.
Yeah, man.

1405
01:18:00,314 --> 01:18:01,215
Thank you.

1406
01:18:02,483 --> 01:18:03,384
So what now?

1407
01:18:05,386 --> 01:18:07,589
Talks of locking down the city.

1408
01:18:07,721 --> 01:18:09,190
Roadblocks at every access.

1409
01:18:09,323 --> 01:18:12,226
Nobody gets in or out
until a second body shows up.

1410
01:18:12,359 --> 01:18:13,394
Maybe I should stay.

1411
01:18:15,396 --> 01:18:18,098
Oh no, you should leave.

1412
01:18:18,232 --> 01:18:20,234
Doesn't take a doctor
to notice that this case has

1413
01:18:20,367 --> 01:18:22,403
been devouring you
from the inside like a cancer

1414
01:18:22,537 --> 01:18:23,904
getting worse by the minute.

1415
01:18:25,306 --> 01:18:26,740
I wouldn't forgive myself
if it pushed you

1416
01:18:26,874 --> 01:18:28,042
to the breaking point.

1417
01:18:31,779 --> 01:18:33,013
Checking out another book?

1418
01:18:33,147 --> 01:18:34,915
Just returning this please.
Okay.

1419
01:18:35,049 --> 01:18:36,884
Did you find what you were
looking for?

1420
01:18:38,052 --> 01:18:39,186
Uh, almost.

1421
01:18:40,821 --> 01:18:44,526
All right. Well, if you
would please just sign

1422
01:18:44,659 --> 01:18:47,328
the log here, first name
and initials.

1423
01:18:48,862 --> 01:18:49,997
Oh, forgot.

1424
01:18:50,130 --> 01:18:51,198
The log's damaged.

1425
01:18:51,332 --> 01:18:53,535
Oh, I will get a new one.

1426
01:18:53,668 --> 01:18:56,837
[FOOTSTEPS RECEDING]

1427
01:18:59,373 --> 01:19:00,374
[SIGHS]

1428
01:19:05,045 --> 01:19:09,783
[SHADING]

1429
01:19:13,454 --> 01:19:14,755
Hey, miss. I--

1430
01:19:14,888 --> 01:19:16,223
Miss, wait.

1431
01:19:17,958 --> 01:19:19,426
It's ironic, isn't it?

1432
01:19:19,561 --> 01:19:21,128
What's that?

1433
01:19:21,262 --> 01:19:24,633
All this evidence,
yet this bracelet still remains

1434
01:19:24,765 --> 01:19:26,267
the biggest mystery.

1435
01:19:26,400 --> 01:19:28,603
You seem fixed on it.
[RUBBER GLOVES SNAPPING OFF]

1436
01:19:28,737 --> 01:19:31,372
You know, I'm done here.
I could--

1437
01:19:31,506 --> 01:19:34,041
I could walk you out
if you want, okay?

1438
01:19:34,174 --> 01:19:35,209
Just give me a minute.

1439
01:19:35,342 --> 01:19:39,213
[WATER RUNNING]

1440
01:19:43,884 --> 01:19:45,486
You ashamed of your scars,
doctor?

1441
01:19:47,388 --> 01:19:50,725
Oh, yeah.
My wife finds it hideous.

1442
01:19:50,858 --> 01:19:52,560
Looks like it hurts.

1443
01:19:52,694 --> 01:19:54,828
Well, they always get
infected this time of year.

1444
01:19:56,765 --> 01:19:59,500
I didn't know healed scars
were prone to reinfection.

1445
01:19:59,634 --> 01:20:01,670
[CHUCKLES]

1446
01:20:01,802 --> 01:20:04,938
[LINE RINGING, BEEPS]

1447
01:20:05,072 --> 01:20:07,609
Kramer, I was wrong.

1448
01:20:07,742 --> 01:20:09,009
It was never the butcher.

1449
01:20:09,143 --> 01:20:10,779
I know who the killer is.

1450
01:20:10,911 --> 01:20:12,514
I'll tell you what,
first thing Monday,

1451
01:20:12,647 --> 01:20:13,947
I'll go through
the inventory again,

1452
01:20:14,081 --> 01:20:15,215
and I'll see if anyone
has anything more

1453
01:20:15,349 --> 01:20:16,850
on that bracelet gift to Gretta.

1454
01:20:16,984 --> 01:20:18,687
What-what-what shape
was that again?

1455
01:20:19,987 --> 01:20:21,055
It's a horseshoe shape.

1456
01:20:23,457 --> 01:20:25,059
But I never told you
it was a gift.

1457
01:20:28,996 --> 01:20:30,632
[DOOR CREAKS OPEN]

1458
01:20:30,765 --> 01:20:31,832
You're the last ones out.

1459
01:20:31,965 --> 01:20:33,467
See you Monday.

1460
01:20:33,601 --> 01:20:34,602
[DOOR SHUTS]

1461
01:20:38,640 --> 01:20:39,973
When I first met them,
they were in need

1462
01:20:40,107 --> 01:20:41,342
of a father figure.

1463
01:20:42,577 --> 01:20:45,012
What else was I supposed to do?

1464
01:20:45,145 --> 01:20:48,315
I swear they were having
just as much fun as I was.

1465
01:20:48,449 --> 01:20:49,883
This was meant to be a game.

1466
01:20:51,285 --> 01:20:52,453
It was our little secret.

1467
01:20:53,987 --> 01:20:55,956
Shame one of them
had to spoil everything.

1468
01:20:56,090 --> 01:20:57,057
[GUN CLICKS]

1469
01:20:57,191 --> 01:20:58,626
[GUNSHOT]
[GRUNTS]

1470
01:20:58,760 --> 01:21:00,127
[EXHALES]

1471
01:21:02,363 --> 01:21:03,531
[GUN FALLS]

1472
01:21:03,665 --> 01:21:06,867
[CLATTERING]

1473
01:21:10,572 --> 01:21:14,509
[LID OPENS]
[HISSING FROM TANK]

1474
01:21:26,554 --> 01:21:27,589
MAN 7:
Dr. Ripley?

1475
01:22:04,158 --> 01:22:05,627
[DOOR CREAKS OPEN]

1476
01:22:10,364 --> 01:22:14,903
[POLICE SIREN WAILING]

1477
01:22:20,775 --> 01:22:21,676
[TRUNK CLOSES]

1478
01:22:25,312 --> 01:22:27,314
[KEYS JANGLE]

1479
01:22:27,448 --> 01:22:33,353
[♪♪♪]

1480
01:22:55,442 --> 01:23:01,348
[♪♪♪]

1481
01:23:03,417 --> 01:23:06,855
[PANTS]

1482
01:23:10,491 --> 01:23:12,226
[SEAT BELT CLICKS]

1483
01:23:22,537 --> 01:23:23,905
ID, please.

1484
01:23:24,037 --> 01:23:26,875
Uh, yes. Uh, officer,
I'm uh, transporting medicine.

1485
01:23:27,007 --> 01:23:28,008
It's-- it's urgent.

1486
01:23:30,210 --> 01:23:31,411
Uh, one moment.

1487
01:23:31,546 --> 01:23:32,680
Please shut off the car.

1488
01:23:32,814 --> 01:23:33,748
Okay.

1489
01:23:34,816 --> 01:23:38,686
[INDISTINCT RADIO CHATTER]

1490
01:23:49,329 --> 01:23:52,399
[PANTS]

1491
01:23:53,801 --> 01:23:56,370
[MIRROR CREAKS]

1492
01:24:06,681 --> 01:24:08,215
Shit.

1493
01:24:08,348 --> 01:24:12,754
[THUMPING]

1494
01:24:26,768 --> 01:24:27,869
[SEAT BELT UNBUCKLES]

1495
01:24:28,002 --> 01:24:31,873
[PANTING, GROANING]

1496
01:24:34,074 --> 01:24:36,276
OFFICER 1:
Sir, I need you
to get back in your vehicle.

1497
01:24:36,410 --> 01:24:38,278
Back in your car.
[GROANS]

1498
01:24:38,412 --> 01:24:39,614
Sir, get back
in your car, please.

1499
01:24:39,747 --> 01:24:40,915
Are you okay?

1500
01:24:41,049 --> 01:24:42,016
Are you all right?

1501
01:24:44,552 --> 01:24:46,086
Give me an EMT up right away.

1502
01:24:46,219 --> 01:24:47,187
Give me an EMT up--

1503
01:24:47,321 --> 01:24:48,523
[GRUNTS]
What?

1504
01:24:48,656 --> 01:24:50,892
[GUNSHOT]
[WOMAN SCREAMING]

1505
01:24:51,025 --> 01:24:52,760
[GUNSHOT]
[THUDS]

1506
01:24:53,928 --> 01:24:56,463
[PANTING]

1507
01:24:58,933 --> 01:25:00,602
[GASPS]

1508
01:25:00,735 --> 01:25:02,036
[HEAVY BREATHING]

1509
01:25:02,169 --> 01:25:03,638
WOMAN 3: Oh my God!
[INDISTINCT SPEECH]

1510
01:25:03,771 --> 01:25:07,307
[PANTING]
[SIRENS WAILING]

1511
01:25:12,245 --> 01:25:16,851
[GUNSHOTS]

1512
01:25:16,985 --> 01:25:18,653
[GASPS]

1513
01:25:18,786 --> 01:25:19,954
Stand down!

1514
01:25:20,088 --> 01:25:21,089
Don't shoot!

1515
01:25:21,221 --> 01:25:23,190
[POLICE SIREN WAILING]

1516
01:25:23,323 --> 01:25:25,994
[TIRES SCREECHING]

1517
01:25:28,796 --> 01:25:30,098
OFFICER 2:
Get to cover. Get back.

1518
01:25:30,230 --> 01:25:31,164
OFFICER 3:
Get your hands...

1519
01:25:31,298 --> 01:25:32,299
[TIRES SCREECHING]

1520
01:25:32,432 --> 01:25:35,202
[PEOPLE SCREAMING]

1521
01:25:35,335 --> 01:25:37,304
[PANTS]

1522
01:25:39,974 --> 01:25:43,845
I never meant to plant evidence
or for anyone to get hurt.

1523
01:25:43,978 --> 01:25:45,780
I don't care why you did it.

1524
01:25:45,913 --> 01:25:48,348
Just give up.

1525
01:25:48,482 --> 01:25:50,518
I can help you survive this.

1526
01:25:53,655 --> 01:25:56,124
[KEYS JANGLE]

1527
01:26:08,136 --> 01:26:10,370
[GUNSHOT]
[PEOPLE SCREAM]

1528
01:26:11,739 --> 01:26:13,173
[GUNSHOTS]
[PEOPLE SCREAM]

1529
01:26:13,306 --> 01:26:15,175
WOMAN 4: Oh my God!
[BACKGROUND CHATTER]

1530
01:26:15,308 --> 01:26:16,744
OFFICER 4:
Suspect is down.

1531
01:26:16,878 --> 01:26:22,850
[INDISTINCT SPEECH]

1532
01:26:22,984 --> 01:26:24,251
Clear the area.
Clear the area.

1533
01:26:24,384 --> 01:26:26,154
OFFICER 5: Officer down.
You're safe.

1534
01:26:27,354 --> 01:26:29,090
You're safe.

1535
01:26:29,222 --> 01:26:30,323
Can I take it?

1536
01:26:32,894 --> 01:26:34,028
Are you hurt?

1537
01:26:41,002 --> 01:26:46,140
[TRAIN MOVING]
[FOOTSTEPS APPROACHING]

1538
01:26:46,273 --> 01:26:48,308
ANNOUNCER [OVER PA]:
<i>Attention all passengers,</i>

1539
01:26:48,442 --> 01:26:50,144
<i>train departing</i>
<i>from platform one will be</i>

1540
01:26:50,277 --> 01:26:52,547
<i>leaving in 10 minutes.</i>

1541
01:26:52,680 --> 01:26:55,248
Didn't I warn you
that a town like this will

1542
01:26:55,382 --> 01:26:57,652
surprise you
in all the worst ways?

1543
01:27:01,022 --> 01:27:02,190
[SIGHS]

1544
01:27:04,224 --> 01:27:06,861
In case like this,
you learn more

1545
01:27:06,994 --> 01:27:10,031
in a week than you would have
with years of training.

1546
01:27:10,164 --> 01:27:12,100
Right. Yeah.

1547
01:27:12,232 --> 01:27:17,772
The Otto family is in lifelong
gratitude to you, as am I.

1548
01:27:17,905 --> 01:27:19,439
Wish I could have done more.

1549
01:27:22,043 --> 01:27:24,444
It's from the town.

1550
01:27:24,579 --> 01:27:26,480
They don't want you
to forget about them.

1551
01:27:27,615 --> 01:27:28,950
Even if I wanted to.

1552
01:27:34,856 --> 01:27:37,992
It's gonna be strange
taking over his office.

1553
01:27:39,694 --> 01:27:40,995
He would have wanted you to.

1554
01:27:44,232 --> 01:27:45,767
I heard about your dissertation.

1555
01:27:48,102 --> 01:27:52,039
Your students, they'll be lucky
to have you at the academy.

1556
01:27:56,244 --> 01:27:58,579
I was hoping to stay
in the field, actually.

1557
01:27:58,713 --> 01:28:00,081
What do you think,
Captain?

1558
01:28:00,214 --> 01:28:02,650
Oh, it's Major now.

1559
01:28:02,784 --> 01:28:04,317
Mm-hmm.

1560
01:28:04,451 --> 01:28:06,453
Got any new cases,
Major?

1561
01:28:06,587 --> 01:28:10,658
Ah, what do you know
about hostage negotiation?

1562
01:28:11,926 --> 01:28:13,426
There's always a first time.

1563
01:28:13,561 --> 01:28:15,129
[LAUGHS]

1564
01:28:16,363 --> 01:28:17,265
New York?

1565
01:28:18,599 --> 01:28:21,169
Well,
your bag's already packed.

1566
01:28:21,301 --> 01:28:22,203
Take your time.

1567
01:28:22,335 --> 01:28:23,604
Think it over.

1568
01:28:23,738 --> 01:28:25,438
No need.

1569
01:28:25,573 --> 01:28:26,707
Tell them
I'm on my way.

1570
01:28:26,841 --> 01:28:32,780
[♪♪♪]

1571
01:28:57,905 --> 01:29:03,778
[♪♪♪]

1572
01:29:19,527 --> 01:29:25,398
[♪♪♪]

1573
01:29:57,965 --> 01:30:03,838
[♪♪♪]

1574
01:30:28,996 --> 01:30:34,835
[♪♪♪]

1575
01:31:00,027 --> 01:31:05,900
[♪♪♪]

1576
01:31:31,025 --> 01:31:36,964
[♪♪♪]